In this work, we address the challenge of extracting flow features, and specifically vortex patterns, from quantum data produced by quantum differential equation solvers. We propose a quantum vortex detection framework based on sliding window operators paired with quantum Fourier analysis. Our method enables spatially localized probes of the vorticity field and parallelized processing of solution patches, avoiding full quantum state tomography. In this work we show that a general strategy for extracting structured information from quantum PDE solutions requires physics-aware approaches and models with only few tunable parameters.


%Vortex identification methods have been extensively studied due to their importance in modeling fluid dynamics. Two approaches are often considered: local and global methods. Local methods derive characteristics from the physical properties of the flow field and apply thresholds to detect the locations of vortices. However, these methods tend to lack robustness because they require pre-defined criteria and thresholds. In contrast, global methods extract geometric or topological features across the entire flow field. While these methods can provide a comprehensive view, they often struggle with scalability for large-scale problems due to their computational intensity. More recently, an algorithm was developed that considered these two methods in unison \cite{swirl_vortex_identification}. The algorithm uses a mathematical criterion to compute the strength of the rotational flow while considering morphological features to identify the center of coherent structures. In addition, \ac{ML} has been used to enhance these vortex detection methods \cite{adaboost_vortex_identification}. Prominent vortex identification methods use \acp{CNN} to perform vortex detection using a combination of local and global methods \cite{vortex_cnn, vortex_seg_net, vortex_elm_net, vortex_u_net}. These methods also look to utilize \ac{ML} as a way to develop automated vortex detection models that are independent of the size and shape of the coherent structures within the fluid flow. \Acp{CNN} are well-suited to recognition problems due to their inherent translational invariance and the locality of the convolutional operator and have been shown to be adaptable for fluid flow problems \cite{cnn_vortex_identification}. However, \acp{CNN} require huge amounts of training data and tend to be purely data-driven, lacking the ability to directly incorporate physical principles.

%\par Given the computational intensity and data-driven nature of classical \ac{ML}-based vortex detection, quantum computing presents a promising alternative. Quantum computing has recently been explored for the detection of coherent vortical structures, offering new possibilities in fluid dynamics analysis. A notable advancement is the introduction of a quantum filament detection model \cite{quantum_filament_detection}. This approach involves creating a quantum state that encodes velocity fields, followed by the extraction of vortex filaments using data-driven \ac{ML} methods. However, it relies on carefully measuring the quantum state and processing the results classically to identify vortex filaments. Extracting vortical features directly from the quantum flow field data remains an open challenge. 

%\par The combination of quantum computing with \ac{ML} holds particular promise for addressing readout challenges inherent in quantum systems, as they allow for the extraction of relevant features without compromising the quantum advantages gained from processing flow field solutions with quantum computing. Obtaining a reduced dimensional representation of the solutions is more efficient than extracting the entire flow field solutions with full state tomography techniques, which face prohibitive measurement overheads \cite{nn_local_state_tomography, Williams2024readout}. The application of \ac{QML} models for the detection of coherent structures presents an opportunity to preserve quantum advantages in data compression while sidestepping the fragility of maintaining coherent quantum states during readout. 

%\par A physics-based \ac{QML} model is developed which performs vortex detection on quantum-generated vorticity field solutions. These solutions are stored as quantum states and can be thought of as quantum data obtained from quantum \ac{PDE} solvers. Such quantum solvers leverage a variety of techniques to accelerate computation and reduce the cost of solving complex \acp{PDE} \cite{quantum_pde_solver_theory, pde_solver_with_cheb, schrodingerisation, exeter_2021, quantum_iterative_solver, pinn_qml}. The model operates by learning a power spectrum representation of the vorticity field, which is then analyzed to identify the presence of vortices. This spectral analysis enables the quantum algorithm to learn the flow behavior encoded within the quantum data. A schematic of this pipeline is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:workflow}. This \ac{QML} framework is concerned with the post-processing of \ac{PDE} solutions obtained directly from quantum algorithms. The incorporation of spectral analysis within \ac{QML} thus provides a physics-informed approach to coherent structure detection that incorporates both data-driven learning and physical insights.

