\section{IV. Conclusion}
\label{Conclusion}

We proposed an algorithm for vortex detection on quantum data, where solutions to differential equations are analyzed with circuits of specific structure. The approach, named quantum vortex detection (QVD), relies on extracting features of flow fields via sliding windows (motivated by convolutional approaches), followed by Fourier analysis along contours to identify signals inherent to structures with vorticity. We described the required sequence of operations for shifting, permuting, and transforming states, and demonstrated QVD on examples that encode Lamb-Oseen vortices. In particular, we showed that vortices can be detected with high accuracy when using a few-parameter model trained to set an optimal detection threshold. Using the coherent sample loading via quantum parallelism, we performed classification of vortical vs non-vortical solutions, greatly outperforming other quantum neural network approaches for quantum data.

As direction for future works we consider extensions of these quantum readout tools to other coherent structures, such as eddies, which are often more chaotic and challenging to detect \cite{eddynet}. Also, it would be beneficial to investigate how this detection model can work in a quantum-inspired regime with differential equation solutions stored as a tensor network \cite{Gourianov2022}. Finally, an intriguing possibility for detecting topological flow features is using quantum methods for topological data analysis \cite{Lloyd2016,Gyurik2022,Berry2024,Scali2024dos,Scali2024thermal}, if modified appropriately for the type of data in question.

%\par Working directly with quantum data, the \ac{QVD} algorithm processes flow field solutions obtained with quantum \ac{CFD} solvers to construct vorticity signals in Fourier space. In particular, the sequential \ac{QVD} algorithm extracts power spectrum features and uses \ac{ML} to identify the locations of vortices, while the parallel \ac{QVD} algorithm extracts density spectrum features and uses \ac{ML} to classify flow behavior. The parallel \ac{QVD} approach can also help to pinpoint the transition to a chaotic regime when used for time-series modeling. Unlike classical \ac{ML} approaches, these \ac{QML} frameworks focus on the post-processing of quantum-generated flow field solutions, preserving the quantum advantage offered by quantum \ac{PDE} solvers while efficiently detecting coherent structures. 

%\par The \ac{QVD} model can be used to detect other types of vortices beyond the Lamb-Oseen vortex model. This may require identifying an alternative signature used to distinguish between the presence or absence of a vortex when analyzing the power spectrum, since different types of vortices may give rise to different distinguishable features. Future work could investigate extending the \ac{QVD} model to other coherent structures, such as eddies, which are often more chaotic and challenging to detect \cite{eddynet}. Additionally, it would be beneficial to investigate how the \ac{QML} model used here can be integrated into a deep learning framework, where different contours and thresholds are optimized in parallel to distinguish different types and sizes of vortices. This would enhance the robustness of the \ac{QVD} model and potentially enable multi-scale modeling for the simultaneous detection of multiple coherent structures.


