\subsection{Heisenberg Model} \label{Heisenberg_section}
Next, we present results of the proposed methods for the 5-qubit cyclic one-dimensional Heisenberg model~\cite{heisenberg_model}. The corresponding Hamiltonian is defined as 
\begin{equation}\label{Heisenberg_hamiltonian}
    H = J   \sum_{i=1}^n \left( X_i X_{i+1} +Y_iY_{i+1} + Z_i Z_{i+1} \right) + h\sum_{i=1}^n Z_i,
\end{equation}
where $J$ is the strength of the spin interaction and $h$ is the magnetic
field strength along the Z-axis. We set $J=h=1$ in this work.

The values for the freeze iteration parameter were set to $\kappa=5,10, 15$ for \verb|Rotosolve|. For \verb|Fraxis| and \verb|FQS|, lower values were used due to a good convergence of these algorithms w.r.t. the number of gate optimizations. That is, we set $\kappa=2,5$ for \verb|Fraxis| and \verb|FQS|. In all experiments, 20 runs were carried out for each iteration value of the freeze $\kappa$ and threshold $T$, as well as the regular versions of the algorithms, which act as a reference to compare how well the gate freezing works. Each run consists of a total of 50 iterations for \verb|Rotosolve|, 50 for \verb|Fraxis|, and 30 for \verb|FQS|. The same experiments were repeated for the incremental increase to the freezing iteration algorithm for all optimizers. We also examine what values the gate freeze iteration vector $\bm{\kappa}$ has after optimizing for each gate $\bm{\kappa}_d$ with different values of $T$ using Algorithm~\autoref{incremental_freeze_algo}. In all the following figures in this subsection, each line in the top row represents a median of 20 runs. In the bottom row, the box plots summarize the results at the end of 20 runs. The boxes span from the first quartile (Q1) to the third quartile (Q3); horizontal bar within each box indicates the median, the diamond marker denotes the mean, and the whiskers represent values within 1.5 times the interquartile range (IQR). The ground state of the Hamiltonian is approximately $E_g=-8.47$.

We provide additional results for incremental gate freeze method in Appendix~\ref{appendix_A} and with different ansatz circuits in Appendix~\ref{appendix_B}.



\subsubsection{Parameter Distances} \label{parameter_results_section}

The results for gate freezing with iterations $\kappa=5, 10, 15$ and incremental gate freezing with \verb|Rotosolve| are shown in Fig.~\ref{rotosolve_heisenberg}. In both subplots in Fig.~\ref{rotosolve_heisenberg}, the gate freezing method outperforms the original base \verb|Rotosolve| in terms of convergence speed and better mean values across the 20 runs. For all freeze iterations $\kappa$, the best median value in each case was $T=0.001$. This outcome was also observed for the incremental freezing method. Subsequently, the next most effective freezing threshold was $T=0.005$ across different values of $\kappa$. However, for the incremental freezing method, a threshold $T=0.01$ yielded better performance. In all experiments, the regular \verb|Rotosolve| without gate freezing had the worst performance of all. 

