\subsubsection{Incremental freezing -- Gate freeze iterations} \label{gate_freeze_iters_section}

In this section, we show our results for individual gates w.r.t. the number of gate freeze iterations $\bm{\kappa}_d$. We examine the average of the incremental gate freezing iterations $\bm{\kappa}$ that exceed the given threshold $T$. After each run, the vector $\bm{\kappa}$ was saved in the data file. We ran a total of 50 runs for each optimizer with the same number of iterations as mentioned at the beginning of Sec. \ref{Results_section}. After that, we take the mean of the gate freezing iterations for each $\bm{\kappa}_d$ and plot the results as a two-dimensional grid, where rows indicate the qubit index of the algorithm and a layer index indicating the layer to which the parameter belongs. The grid can be interpreted as representing the positions of the parameters in the circuit. That is, the leftmost column corresponds to the first gates optimized during each iteration, while the rightmost column represents the last gates optimized in the circuit. The columns can be thought of as parameter layers in a PQC. Each block in the grid represents the mean of the gate freeze iterations of 50 runs. Red cells indicate higher averages, while blue cells correspond to lower averages. For all optimizers, the angle distance between the parameters was used as a metric.

We show the results for \verb|Rotosolve| in Fig.~\ref{roto_index_inc_freeze_results}. Here, we have run a total of 50 runs with 3 layers. In the plots, there are a total of six columns, corresponding to one circuit ansatz layer consisting of $R_X$ and $R_Y$ gates applied to each qubit. This increases the number of parameters in the circuit by twice the number of actual layers. The color of the grid squares is the average gate freeze iteration parameter $\bm{\kappa}_d$ for each gate over the 50 runs. The red color means a higher $\bm{\kappa}_d$ and blue a lower $\bm{\kappa}_d$ average value. That is, the red grid squares have been frozen more often and for longer during optimization than the blue grid squares. 

