\section{Mutual Information Between the Qubits in Different Ansätze}\label{appendix_mutual_info}

This section presents an analysis of mutual information heat maps between qubits for different ansatz circuit types A--D, each consisting of 5 qubits and 3 layers. This investigation aims to provide a more accurate metric for evaluating correlations observed in the gate freezing heat maps. Ansatz circuit type A is shown in Fig.~\ref{ansatz_circuit}, while types B--D are provided in Appendix~\ref{appendix_C}. \\

The mutual information between two quantum subsystems, $P$ and $Q$, forming a joint state described by the bipartite density matrix $\rho_{PQ}$, is defined as the real-valued quantity $I(P:Q)$~\cite{nielsen2010quantum}. It is computed as:
\begin{equation}
    I(P:Q) = S(\rho_P) + S(\rho_Q) - S(\rho_{PQ}),
\end{equation}
where $S(\rho_P)$ denotes the von Neumann entropy, defined as $S(\rho_P) = -\Tr\left[\rho_P \log \rho_P \right]$ and $\rho_P$, and $\rho_Q$ are the reduced density matrices of $\rho_{PQ}$~\cite{nielsen2010quantum}. 

Mutual information quantifies the total correlations, both classical and quantum, between subsystems $P$ and $Q$. It serves as a key tool for understanding the structural and behavioral properties of quantum circuits~\cite{tisoc2022mutual, modi_quantum_corr}. Unlike measures that capture only quantum correlations, such as entanglement entropy, mutual information includes all forms of correlation and therefore serves as an upper bound to more specific quantities like entanglement of formation or quantum discord~\cite{Henderson_2001, horodecki2005partial, de2018genuine}. In the context of PQCs, mutual information offers insight into a circuit's expressibility and entangling capability~\cite{sim2019expressibility, anand2024information}. Evaluating mutual information between all pairs of qubits across different ansatz types reveals how quantum correlations are distributed and how information propagates through the circuit. High mutual information between distant qubits may indicate long-range entanglement or strong connectivity, potentially impacting the performance of VQAs~\cite{holmes2022connecting}. Next, we present our results on mutual information between qubits of the PQC for ansatz types A--D. 

We conducted our analysis by drawing $10^4$ samples for each ansatz type using the \verb|Rotosolve|, \verb|Fraxis|, and \verb|FQS| optimizers, all configured with 3 layers. In each sample, circuit parameters were sampled from the uniform distribution associated with each optimizer, as described in Sec~\ref{Results_section}. For each sampled circuit, we obtained the output quantum state, computed the corresponding density matrix, and evaluated the mutual information matrix across all qubit pairs. The final heat maps represent the average mutual information values between qubit pairs across all samples. The results for \verb|Rotosolve|, \verb|Fraxis| and \verb|FQS| are shown in Fig.~\ref{mutual_info_plot_rotosolve}, Fig.~\ref{mutual_info_plot_Fraxis} and Fig.~\ref{mutual_info_plot_FQS}, respectively.

