    \section{Introduction}
    \label{sec:intro}

    The families of abelian varieties defined over a global field are fundamental. In this paper, we study families of elliptic curves over function fields. By a family, we mean specifically an \textit{elliptic fibration}, that is an algebraic surface $X$ that admits a proper flat morphism $f : X \to C$ to a smooth projective curve $C/k$ over a field $k$ such that a general fiber is a smooth curve of genus one. Such an $X$ is sometimes called an \emph{elliptic surface} in other literature. It is natural to work with the case when there exists a distinguished section $s: C \hookrightarrow X$ coming from the identity points on each of the elliptic fibers. An elliptic fibration is called \textit{relatively-minimal} if none of the fibers contain any $(-1)$-curves.

    \medskip

    It is natural to ask how many elliptic fibrations $f: X \to C$ exist. This question is equivalent to determining the total number of rational points on $\Me$ over a function field $K = k(C)$. For proper stacks, unlike schemes, there is a distinction between rational and integral points. Moreover, rational points have extra automorphism groups. In the case where $K = \Fb_q(t)$ with $\text{char} (\Fb_q) > 3$, the exact number of isomorphism classes of elliptic curves over $K$ is established in \cite[Theorem 9.7]{BPS}. The proof relies on the height moduli framework developed in \cite[Theorems 1.2 \& 5.1]{BPS} by Bejleri, Satriano, and the author. Specifically, the method involves extracting coefficients of rational motivic height zeta functions $Z_{\vec{\lambda}}(t)$ associated to the height moduli spaces and their variants on the corresponding inertia stacks $\Ic Z_{\vec{\lambda}}(t)$ as described in \cite[Theorem 8.9]{BPS}. In the present work, we extend the enumerations to the remaining cases $\text{char} (\Fb_q) = 2,3$ inspired by the classical work of \cite{dJ2}.

    \medskip

    Specifically, we establish the following sharp enumeration of elliptic curves over a global function field $K = \Fb_q(t)$ with precise lower order main terms. Recall that the height of the discriminant of an elliptic curve $E$ over $K$ is given by $ht(\Delta):=q^{\deg \Delta} = q^{12n}$ for some integer $n$ (also called the Faltings height of $E$).

    \begin{thm}\label{thm:ell_curve_count_char_32}
    Let $n \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$. The counting function $\Nc^w(\Fb_q(t),~B)$ (resp. $\Nc(\Fb_q(t), ~B)$), which gives the weighted count (resp. unweighted count) of the number of isomorphism classes of minimal elliptic curves over $\Pb^1_{\Fb_q}$ ordered by the multiplicative height of the discriminant $ht(\Delta) = q^{12n} \le B$, is given by the following.

    \begin{align*}
    \Nc^w(\Fb_q(t),~B) &= \left( \frac{q^{9} - 1}{q^{8} - q^{7}} \right) B^{5/6} - B^{1/6}
    \end{align*}

    \begin{enumerate}
    \item For $q = 3^r$ \\

    \begin{itemize}

        \item r is odd :
        \begin{align*}
         \Nc(\Fb_q(t),~B) &= 2 \left( \frac{q^{9} - 1}{q^{8} - q^{7}} \right) B^{5/6} - 2 B^{1/6}   \\
        & + 2 \left( \frac{q^{7}-1}{q^{7}-q^{6}} \right) B^{2/3} -  2 \left( \frac{q^{3} - 1}{q^{4}-q^3} \right) B^{1/3} \\
        \end{align*}

        \item r is even :
        \begin{align*}
         \Nc(\Fb_q(t),~B) &= 2 \left( \frac{q^{9} - 1}{q^{8} - q^{7}} \right) B^{5/6} - 2 B^{1/6}   \\
        & + 4 \left( \frac{q^{7}-1}{q^{7}-q^{6}} \right) B^{2/3} -  4 \left( \frac{q^{3} - 1}{q^{4}-q^3} \right) B^{1/3} \\
        \end{align*}

    \end{itemize}

    \item For $q = 2^r$ \\

    \begin{itemize}

        \item r is odd :
        \begin{align*}
         \Nc(\Fb_q(t),~B) &= 2 \left( \frac{q^{9} - 1}{q^{8} - q^{7}} \right) B^{5/6} - 2 B^{1/6} \\
        & + \left( \frac{q^8 - 1}{q^8 - q^7} \right) B^{3/4} -  \left( \frac{q^5 - 1}{q^6 - q^5} \right) B^{1/2} \\
        & - 2q + 4 \\
        \end{align*}

        \item r is even :
        \begin{align*}
         \Nc(\Fb_q(t),~B) &= 2 \left( \frac{q^{9} - 1}{q^{8} - q^{7}} \right) B^{5/6} - 2 B^{1/6} \\
        & + 5 \left( \frac{q^8 - 1}{q^8 - q^7} \right) B^{3/4} - 5 \left( \frac{q^5 - 1}{q^6 - q^5} \right) B^{1/2} \\
        & - 2q + 4 \\
        \end{align*}

    \end{itemize}

    \end{enumerate}

    \end{thm}

    \begin{rmk}
    The lower order main term of order $B^{1/6}$ present in both the weighted and unweighted counts comes from subtracting the $\mu_2$ twist families of generically singular isotrivial $j = \infty$ elliptic curves. And the lower order main terms of order $B^{2/3}$ \& $B^{1/3}$ for $\text{char}(\Fb_q) = 3$ and $B^{3/4}$ \& $B^{1/2}$ for $\text{char}(\Fb_q) = 2$ in the unweighted count $\Nc(\Fb_q(t),~B)$ come from counting the $Q_{12}$ and $Q_{24}$ twist families of isotrivial elliptic curves having strictly additive bad reductions with extra automorphisms concentrated at the supersingular $j$-invariant $j = 0$.
    \end{rmk}

    \subsection{Outline of the paper} 
    In Section \ref{sec:motive_ptcts}, we compute the motivic classes in the Grothendieck ring of stacks of the inertia stack $\Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ for $\mathrm{char}(k) \ge 0$ and height moduli in the case of weighted projective stacks. In Section \ref{sec:Ell_32}, we count the exact unweighted number of rational points on the classifying stacks $\mathcal{B} Q_{12}$, $\mathcal{B} Q_{24}$ and $\mathcal{B} Z$, where $Q_{12}$ and $Q_{24}$ denote the dicyclic groups of orders 12 and 24, respectively, and $Z$ denotes the non-reduced group scheme of order 2. Afterward, we enumerate elliptic curves over $\Fb_q(t)$ with $\text{char}(\Fb_q) = 3,2$ and prove Theorem \ref{thm:ell_curve_count_char_32}.

    \medskip

    \section{Motives \& Point counts over finite fields}
    \label{sec:motive_ptcts}

    In this section, we briefly review the arithmetic of algebraic stacks over a perfect field $k$, with a focus on the case when $k = \Fb_q$ is a finite field. Afterward, we introduce motivic invariants of moduli stacks via the Grothendieck ring $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ of $k$-stacks. We compute the motives of $\mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ and its inertia stack $\Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1}$. We also recall the height moduli spaces on $0$-dimensional weighted projective stack $\Pc(a)$ for later computation of the motives of height moduli spaces on the classifying stacks $\mathcal{B} Q_{12}$, $\mathcal{B} Q_{24}$ and $\mathcal{B} Z$, where $Q_{12}$ and $Q_{24}$ denote the dicyclic groups of orders 12 and 24, respectively, and $Z$ denotes the non-reduced group scheme of order 2.

    \medskip

    Due to the presence of automorphisms, point counts of an algebraic stack $\cX$ over finite fields are weighted.

    \begin{defn}\label{def:wtcount}
        The weighted point count of an algebraic stack $\cX$ with finite inertia over $\Fb_q$ is defined as a sum
        \[
        \#_q(\cX)\coloneqq\sum_{x \in \cX(\Fb_q)/\sim}\frac{1}{|\mathrm{Aut}(x)|},
        \]
        where $\cX(\Fb_q)/\sim$ is the set of $\Fb_q$--isomorphism classes of $\Fb_q$--points of $\cX$.
    \end{defn}

    The main advantage of the weighted point count is that it is \textit{algebro-topological} as it depends only on the cohomology of $\cX$ and is equal to the usual point count of the coarse moduli space via the Grothendieck-Lefschetz trace formula for algebraic stacks proven by classical works of \cite{Behrend,Sun}.

    \medskip

    It is important to note that the above sum runs over the set $\cX(\Fb_q)/\sim$ of isomorphism classes over $\Fb_q$ thus the weighted point count $\#_q(\cX)$ is \textit{not equal} to the number $|\cX(\Fb_q)/\sim|$ of $\Fb_q$--isomorphism classes when there is a non-trivial automorphism $|\mathrm{Aut}(x)| \neq 1$ for some stacky point $x \in \cX(\Fb_q)/\sim$. Because of this, for enumeration purposes, it is important to consider the unweighted count of isomorphism classes. The following result of \cite{HP2} shows that the unweighted point count is also natural and depends on the arithmetic of the inertia stack of $\cX$.

    \begin{thm}[Theorem 1.1. of \cite{HP2}] \label{thm:ptcounts}
        Let $\cX$ be an algebraic stack over $\Fb_q$ of finite type with quasi-separated finite type diagonal and let $\Ic(\cX)$ be the inertia stack of $\cX$. Then, 
        $$ |\cX(\Fb_q)/\sim| = \#_q(\Ic(\cX))$$ 
    \end{thm}

    In this paper, we study proportions via motivic classes in the Grothendieck ring $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ of $k$-stacks. We review some properties of the Grothendieck ring of stacks introduced in \cite{Ekedahl}. 

    \begin{defn}\label{defn:GrothringStck}
        \cite[\S 1]{Ekedahl}
        The \emph{Grothendieck ring of stacks} $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ is the abelian group generated by classes $\{\cX\}_k$ for each algebraic stack $\cX$ of finite type over $k$ with affine inertia modulo the relations
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\{\cX\}_k=\{\Zc\}_k+\{\cX \setminus \Zc\}_k$ for $\Zc \subset \cX$ a closed substack,
            \item $\{\Ec\}_k=\{\cX \times_k \Ab^n \}_k$ for $\Ec$ a vector bundle of rank $n$ on $\cX$.
        \end{itemize}
        Multiplication on $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ is induced by $\{\cX\}_k\{\Yc\}_k\coloneqq\{\cX \times_k \Yc\}$. 
        There is a distinguished element $\Lb \coloneqq\{\A^1\}_k \in K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$, called the \emph{Lefschetz motive}. We drop the subscript if $k$ is clear. 
    \end{defn}

    We denote by $K_0'(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ the ring obtained by imposing only the cut-and-paste relation but not the vector bundle relation and denote the class of a stack in this ring by $\{\cX\}'$. The Grothendieck ring is universal among all additive and multiplicative invariants. For instance, when $k=\Fb_q$, the point counting measure $\{\cX\} \mapsto \#_q(\cX)$ is a well-defined ring homomorphism $\#_q: K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_{\Fb_q}) \rightarrow \Q$ giving the weighted point count $\#_q(\cX)$ of $\cX$ over $\Fb_q$. When $\{\cX\}$ is mixed Tate i.e. a polynomial in the Lefschetz motive $\Lb \coloneqq\{\A^1_{k}\}$ which serves as a natural ``measure’’ of the affine line, the weighted point count is a polynomial in $q$.

    %Notably, this approach extends beyond finite fields to cases where the perfect residue field $k$ lacks an additive invariant, such as $K = \Q(t)$ for $k = \Q$.

    \medskip

    Recall that an algebraic group $G$ is \textit{special} in the sense of \cite{Serre} and \cite{Grothendieck}, if every $G$-torsor is Zariski-locally trivial; for example $\Gb_a,~\GL_{d},~\SL_{d}$ are special and $\PGL_2,~\PGL_3$ are non-special. If $\mathcal X \rightarrow \mathcal Y$ is a $G$-torsor and $G$ is special, then we have $\{\mathcal X\} = \{G\} \cdot \{\mathcal Y\}$ (\cite[Prop. 1.1 iii)]{Ekedahl}). 

    \medskip

    Finally, we can use the following result to access unweighted point counts. 

    \begin{prop}{\cite[Prop. 5.3]{Fernex}}\label{prop:df} The association $\cX \mapsto \Ic \cX$ extends to a unique ring homomorphism 
    $$
    \Ic : K_0'(\mathrm{Stck}_k) \to K_0'(\mathrm{Stck}_k)
    $$
    which we call the inertia operator. 
    \end{prop}

    Note that $\Ic$ does not descend to a well defined operator on $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ as in \cite[Prop. 1.1 iii)]{Ekedahl}. In order to keep track of the primitive roots of unity contained in $\mathbb{F}_q$, we define the following auxiliary function.

        \[
        \delta(x)\coloneqq
        \begin{cases}
            1 & \text{if } x \text{ divides } q-1,\\
            0 & \text{otherwise.}
        \end{cases}
        \]

    \begin{prop} Let $k$ be a perfect field with $\mathrm{char}(k) \nmid a,b$. The motivic classes of the weighted projective stack $\Pc(a,b)$ and its inertia stack $\Ic \Pc(a,b)$ in $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ are equal to
    $$\left\{ \Pc(a,b) \right\} = \Lb+1 $$
    $$\left\{ \Ic \Pc(a,b) \right\} = \gcd(a,b) \cdot (\Lb+1) + \delta(a) \cdot (a-\gcd(a,b)) + \delta(b) \cdot (b-\gcd(a,b))$$
    \end{prop}

    \begin{proof}
    As $\Pc(a,b) \coloneqq \left[(\Ab^2 \setminus \{0\})/\Gb_m \right]$ we have $\left\{ \Pc(a,b) \right\} = \frac{\Lb^{2} - 1}{\Lb - 1} = \Lb + 1$ which holds as $\Gb_m = \GL_1$ is a special group. For the inertia stack $\Ic \Pc(a,b)$, note that $$\Ic \Pc(a,b) = \sqcup_{\gcd(a,b)} \Pc(a,b) \sqcup_{a-\gcd(a,b)} \Pc(a) \sqcup_{b-\gcd(a,b)} \Pc(b)$$ by \cite[Proposition 3.5]{HP2} which translates to $$\left\{ \Ic \Pc(a,b) \right\} = \gcd(a,b) \cdot \left\{ \Pc(a,b) \right\} + \delta(a) \cdot (a-\gcd(a,b)) \cdot \left\{ \Pc(a) \right\} + \delta(b) \cdot (b-\gcd(a,b)) \cdot \left\{ \Pc(b) \right\}$$ As $\Pc(r) \coloneqq \left[(\Ab^{1} \setminus \{0\})/\Gb_m \right]$ hence $\left\{ \Pc(r) \right\} = \frac{\Lb - 1}{\Lb - 1} = 1$ we get the desired formula.
    \end{proof}

    %If the underlying field $k$ is not a finite field but instead $k=\Cb$, the mixed-Tate motive of the inertia stack reflects the topology of the underlying stack as a polynomial in $\Lb$. 

    Note that computing the exact weighted point count $\#_q(\Ic(\cX))$ of the inertia stack via algebro-topological method is useful as we are able to deduce the exact unweighted point count of the underlying stack $|\cX(\Fb_q)/\sim|$. 

    \medskip

    Let us illustrate this important discrepancy with an example. One can ask how many isomorphism classes of elliptic curves are there over $\Fb_q$. 

    \begin{prop}\label{prop:M11mot}
    Let $k$ be a perfect field. The motivic class of the fine modular curve $\mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ of smooth elliptic curves in $K_0(\mathrm{Stck}_k)$ is equal to 

    \begin{align*}
            \left\{ \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} &= \Lb \\
    \end{align*}

    The motivic classes of the inertia stack $\Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ for $\mathrm{char}(k) \neq 2,3$ is equal to 

        \begin{align*}
            \left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} &= 2\Lb + \delta(6) \cdot 4 + \delta(4) \cdot 2\\
        \end{align*}

    which translates to the following for $k = \Fb_q$ with $\mathrm{char}(\Fb_q) \neq 2,3$

        \begin{align*}
            \left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} 
            &= 2\Lb+6,~~\text{if } q \equiv 1 \mod 12,\\
            &= 2\Lb+2,~~\text{if } q \equiv 5 \mod 12 ,\\
            &= 2\Lb+4,~~\text{if } q \equiv 7 \mod 12 ,\\
            &= 2\Lb,~~\text{if } q \equiv 11 \mod 12.\\
        \end{align*}

    The motivic classes of the inertia stack $\Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ for $\mathrm{char}(\Fb_q) = 2,3$ is equal to

        \begin{align*}
            \left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} 
            &= 2\Lb+1,~~\text{if } q = 2^r \text{ with r odd, }\\
            &= 2\Lb+5,~~\text{if } q = 2^r \text{ with r even, }\\
            &= 2\Lb+2,~~\text{if } q = 3^r \text{ with r odd, }\\
            &= 2\Lb+4,~~\text{if } q = 3^r \text{ with r even. }
        \end{align*}

    \end{prop}

    \begin{proof}
    The weighted point count is identical over any field which follows from the coarse moduli space $\CMe \iso \Pb^1$ over $\Spec \Zb$ thus we have $\left\{ \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} = \left\{ \CMe - \{j=\infty\} \right\} = (\Lb + 1) - 1 = \Lb$. The classes $\left\{ \Ic (\mathcal{M}_{1,1} - \{j=0\} - \{j=1728\})  \right\} = 2(\Lb-2)$ of the inertia stack for $\mathrm{char}(k) \neq 3,2$ follows as the automorphism group of a geometric point of $\mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ is of order 2 away from $j=0$ and $j=1728$ (c.f. \cite[Ap. A, Prop. 1.2]{Silverman}). It remains to find out how many $\Fb_q$--points $j=0, 1728$ have respectively depending on the primitive roots of unity contained in $\mathbb{F}_q$. This is $\delta(6) \cdot 6$ for $j=0$ and $\delta(4) \cdot 4$ for $j=1728$.

    For the classes of the inertia stack for $\text{char} (\Fb_q) = 3,2$, the classes $\left\{ \Ic (\mathcal{M}_{1,1} - \{j=0\} \right\} = 2(\Lb-1)$ of the inertia stack follows as the automorphism group of a geometric point of $\mathcal{M}_{1,1}$ is of order 2 away from $j=0$ (c.f. \cite[Ap. A, Prop. 1.2]{Silverman}). For $q = 3^r$, It remains to find out how many $\Fb_q$--points $j=0$ has which is the number of $\Fb_q$--isomorphism classes of supersingular elliptic curves. For r odd we have 4 isomorphism classes and for r even we have 6 isomorphism classes from \cite[Thm. 3.5]{CJ}. Thus we have $\left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} = 2(\Lb-1) + 4$ for r odd and $\left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} = 2(\Lb-1) + 6$ for r even. Similarly for the number of $\Fb_q$--isomorphism classes of supersingular elliptic curves for $q = 2^r$, for r odd we have 3 isomorphism class and for r even we have 7 isomorphism classes from \cite[Thm. 3.6 \& 3.7]{Menezes}. Thus we have $\left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} = 2(\Lb-1) + 1$ for r odd and $\left\{ \Ic \mathcal{M}_{1,1} \right\} = 2(\Lb-1) + 5$ for r even. 

    \end{proof}

