This challenge is particularly important because, as psychological research demonstrates, procedural learning is inherently more difficult than conceptual learning \cite{Byrnes1991-ug}. The implications are universal: whether in scientific research, technical workflows, or organizational operations, the ability to encode, retrieve, and compare procedural knowledge could unlock new paradigms in how one documents, teaches, and builds upon processes.

