	\title{\bf Sure Convergence and Constructive Universal Approximation for Multi-Layer Neural Networks}

	\author{Chien-Ming Chi\thanks{
			Chien-Ming Chi is Assistant Research Fellow, Institute of Statistical Science, Academia Sinica, Taipei 11529, Taiwan (Email: \textit{xbbchi@stat.sinica.edu.tw}). %
			This work was supported by grant 113-2118-M-001-008-MY2 from the National Science and Technology Council, Taiwan.	}
		\hspace{.2cm}\\
		Academia Sinica}
	\date{}
	\maketitle





	\begin{abstract}
		We propose a new neural network model, 01Neuro, built on indicator activation neurons. Its boosted variant possesses two key statistical properties: (1) Sure Convergence, where model optimization can be achieved with high probability given sufficient computational resources; and (2) Constructive Universal Approximation: In the infinite-sample setting, the model can approximate any finite sum of measurable functions, each depending on only $k$ out of $p$ input features, provided the architecture is properly tuned. Unlike most universal approximation results that are agnostic to training procedures, our guarantees are directly tied to the model’s explicit construction and optimization algorithm. To improve prediction stability, we integrate  stochastic training and bagging into the boosted 01Neuro framework. Empirical evaluations on simulated and real-world tabular datasets with small to medium sample sizes highlight its strengths: effective approximation of interaction components (multiplicative terms), stable prediction performance (comparable to Random Forests), robustness to many noisy features ($p = 100$, $n \le 450$), and insensitivity to feature scaling. A major limitation of the current implementation of boosted 01Neuro is its higher computational cost, which is approximately 5 to 30 times that of Random Forests and XGBoost.
	\end{abstract}

	\noindent%

	\textit{Running title}: Sure Convergence and Constructive Universal Approximation

	\textit{Keywords}: Boosted greedy approximation; sparse neural networks; exploit–explore optimization;  active and idle neurons; nonconvex optimization problems.


	\section{Introduction}


Predictive models based on neural networks \citep{lecun2015deep} lie at the core of recent artificial intelligence advancements, including AlphaGo \citep{silver2016mastering}, GPT-3 \citep{brown2020language}, and AlphaFold \citep{jumper2021highly}. Their high prediction accuracy has been extensively studied and supported from theoretical perspectives, spanning from approximation results for single-hidden-layer networks \citep{hornik1989multilayer, cybenko1989approximation, barron1993universal} to advanced universal approximation theories for multilayer networks \citep{bauer2019deep, schmidt2020nonparametric}.



However, despite their strong empirical performance and universal approximation ability, neural networks can sometimes fall short in practice. Numerous formal studies have shown that neural networks may not outperform other prediction methods on tabular data \citep{grinsztajn2022tree, mcelfresh2023neural, shwartz2022tabular}. In particular, multilayer neural networks have been found to be consistently outperformed by tree-based models \citep{grinsztajn2022tree}, a result that appears to conflict with their universal approximation capabilities. This gap between theory and practice has been a subject of extensive investigation \citep{adcock2021gap, grohs2024proof}. A plausible explanation for this gap may be the failure to achieve complete model optimization, a condition that universal approximation theory assumes \citep{schmidt2020nonparametric, bauer2019deep} but is rarely guaranteed in practice \citep{fan2020selective}.
Whether neural networks inherently possess universal approximation capability by construction, often called constructive approximation \citep{gentile2024approximation}, or how this ability might be effectively realized, remains a significant open problem.


At least three main approaches have been pursued to address this problem. First, adaptive gradient methods such as Adam and other variants \citep{kingma2015adam, hinton2012neural, duchi2011adaptive} have been proposed and recently shown to converge under certain conditions \citep{zhang2022adam, li2023convergence, defossez2020simple}. Nevertheless, both theoretical \citep{jentzen2024non} and empirical \citep{choromanska2015loss} studies indicate that gradient-based optimization often converges to local minima for which universal approximation is not guaranteed. Second, from the perspectives of neural tangent kernels \citep{jacot2018neural, xu2024overparametrized} or mean field theory \citep{mei2018mean, mei2019mean}, sufficiently wide and often impractically large neural networks can be optimized using gradient methods. However, several key theoretical assumptions limit the practical applicability of these results for understanding neural network optimization. The third approach attempts to develop constructive approximation theory for neural networks \citep{gentile2024approximation, arora2019fine}. While gradient-based update methods and network architectures have been appropriately considered, these related theories are still in their early stages, with current results limited to single-hidden-layer or shallow networks.



This paper makes a promising contribution to the constructive approximation of neural networks by developing a new network model called 01Neuro. The new network utilizes indicator activation functions instead of standard smooth ones, adopts a sparse structure with both active and idle neurons, and is trained using Exploit-Explore Optimizing, a newly proposed method that iteratively updates its weights and biases. When the width of 01Neuro is properly tuned, the network includes a sufficient number of idle neurons, which do not directly influence the output but allow the model to explore alternative subnetworks. This helps eliminate the risk of becoming trapped in local minima during optimization. We formally prove the sure convergence property of the boosted variant~\citep{friedman2001greedy} of 01Neuro, demonstrating that its weight and bias optimization through Exploit-Explore Optimizing can be practically completed in a probabilistic sense, given sufficient computational resources. In other words, the proposed model offers a principled solution to nonconvex optimization in network training. We further establish constructive universal approximation for the proposed model, showing that boosted 01Neuro can approximate any finite sum of measurable functions, each depending on $k$ out of $p$ coordinates, with sufficient network depth ($L \ge 2 + \lceil \log_{2}k   \rceil$) and an appropriate level of sparsity.





We empirically demonstrate the universal approximation capability of the proposed model in Section~\ref{Sec6} using small-scale simulated data with numerous noisy features. In Section~\ref{Sec7}, we further compare boosted 01Neuro with two popular tree-based models, XGBoost \citep{chen2016xgboost} and Random Forests \citep{breiman2001random}, to evaluate their prediction performance \citep{gribonval2001approximate, mcelfresh2023neural}. The version of boosted 01Neuro evaluated in these two sections is trained with the practical training techniques presented in Section~\ref{Sec5}. Our empirical results suggest that the proposed model offers four practical advantages: effective approximation of complex components (multiplicative terms), stable prediction performance (comparable to Random Forests), robustness to numerous noisy features ($p = 100$, $n \le 450$), and insensitivity to feature scaling. One potential drawback of the current implementation is its comparatively high computational cost, which is approximately 5 to 30 times greater than that of the slower of Random Forests and XGBoost.



Our proposed model may be particularly appealing, for example, to researchers aiming to develop constructive and well-grounded neural network inference methods, and to practitioners looking to develop neural network–based feature representation techniques for applications with limited sample sizes or irregular input features.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{Sec2} introduces the architecture of boosted 01Neuro, while Section~\ref{Sec4.1} presents Exploit-Explore Optimizing and its sure convergence property. In Section~\ref{Sec4.new}, we establish the constructive universal approximation theory for the proposed model. 
All technical proofs are deferred to the online Supplementary Material. 





\subsection{Notation}


Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$ be a probability space. Random vectors are denoted by bold-faced symbols, such as $\boldsymbol{X}$ or $\boldsymbol{X}_j$, while random variables are denoted by $Y$, $Z$, or $X_j$. A constant $k$-dimensional vector is represented by $\vv{x} = (x_1, \dots, x_k)^{\top} \in \mathbb{R}^k$, with its squared $L_2$-norm defined by $\norm{\vv{x}}_2^2 = \sum_{j=1}^k x_j^2$ and its $L_0$-norm by $\norm{\vv{x}}_0 = \texttt{\#} \{j : |x_j| > 0\}$. The indicator function, denoted by $\boldsymbol{1}\{\cdot\}$, equals 1 if its condition is true and 0 otherwise. The smallest integer greater than $x$ is denoted by $\lceil x \rceil$, while the largest integer less than $x$ is denoted by $\lfloor x \rfloor$.








