	\section{Sparse Neural Networks with Indicator Neurons}\label{Sec2}

    % an input layer with \( p \) features,   composed of \( M \) boosting output functions

	The neural network considered in this paper, 01Neuro, maps \( p \)-dimensional input features to a \(  q \)-dimensional output. The name 01Neuro reflects the use of indicator activation functions, which produce binary feature representations throughout the network's forward pass. The architecture of an 01Neuro consists of \( L - 1 \) hidden layers and an output layer. Each hidden layer $l \in \{1, \dots, L-1\}$ contains $p_l$ neurons, and the output layer consists of a single neuron with $p_L = 1$. For simplicity, in this paper, we consider the applications with \( q = 1 \).




    At the first hidden layer, for each $h\in \{1, \dots, p\}$ and each $\vv{x}\in[0, 1]^p$, define    
			$f_{1, h}(\vv{x}) = \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{w}_{1, h}^{\top}\vv{x} > c_{1, h}\}$ with 
            $\vv{f}_{1}(\vv{x}) = (f_{1, 1}(\vv{x}), \dots, f_{1, p}(\vv{x}) )^{\top}$.	
    For $l\in \{2, \dots, L-1\}$ and $h\in \{1, \dots, p_{l}\}$, define the output function of the $h$th neural net at the $l$th layer  $f_{l, h}:\mathbb{R}^{p} \longmapsto \{0, 1\}$ by
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{split}
			f_{l, h}(\vv{x}) & = \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{w}_{l, h}^{\top}\vv{f}_{l-1}(\vv{x}) > c_{l, h}\},\\
			\vv{f}_{l}(\vv{x}) & = (f_{l, 1}(\vv{x}), \dots, f_{l, p_{l}}(\vv{x}) )^{\top}.
		\end{split}    
	\end{equation*}
	Here, \(\vv{w}_{l, h} \in \{ \vv{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{p_{l-1}} : \|\vv{w}\|_2 = 1 \}\) with $p_{0} \coloneqq p$ representing a weight vector of unit length, and \(c_{l,h} \in \mathbb{R}\) representing a bias. Additionally, let $f_{L} : \mathbb{R}^{p}\longmapsto \{0, 1\}$ be such that
	$$f_{L}(\vv{x}) = \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{w}_{L, 1}^{\top}\vv{f}_{L-1}(\vv{x}) > c_{L, 1}\},$$
	for some  $\vv{w}_{L, 1} \in \mathbb{R}^{p_{L-1}}$ of unit length. Weight vectors are assumed to be sparse such that $\norm{\vv{w}_{l, h}}_0 \le w_0$ for each $l\in\{1, \dots, L\}$ and each $h\in \{1, \dots, p_{l}\}$, with some integer parameter $w_0 >0$. In our sparse networks, many neuron functions (also called neuron units or simply neurons) do not contribute to the final output $f_L$ and are referred to as idle neurons, while those that do affect the output are called active neurons. Section~\ref{Sec4.1} explains how weight exploration is guided by the utilization of idle neurons. Figure~\ref{fig:sparsen1} illustrates an example of a 01Neuro with calibrated parameters, as listed in Table~\ref{tab:basic_table} of Section~\ref{SecA.2}, that approximates a diamond-shaped region.




	\begin{figure}[h]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{sparseneuro1.png}
		\caption{An example of 01Neuro with $L = 3, p = 3, p_1 = 8, p_2 = 4, p_3 = 1$, and $w_0 = 2$. In the left panel, dashed lines indicate inactive neuron functions, while solid lines indicate active ones. The right panel illustrates how 01Neuro progressively transforms the 3-dimensional input features \( \vec{x} =(x_1, x_2, x_3)^{\top}\) into a diamond-shaped output function on the \(x_1\)–\(x_2\) plane. The red region indicates where an indicator neuron function $f_{l, h}$ takes the value one.}
		\label{fig:sparsen1}
	\end{figure}



	We now introduce the boosted 01Neuro model, following the approach of \citep{friedman2001greedy}.  Define 
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{split}
			\label{ilw}
			\mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} & = \{g: g(\vv{x}) = \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}^{\top}\vv{f}(\vv{x}) > a\}  , \vv{f} \in \mathcal{F}_{L-1, w_0}, \vv{u}\in \mathbb{R}^{p_{L-1}}, \\
			& \quad \norm{\vv{u}}_{2}=1, \norm{\vv{u}}_{0}\le w_0, a  \in \mathbb{R} \}
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	where $\mathcal{F}_{L-1, w_0} = \{  \vv{f}_{L-1} :  \vv{w}_{l, h} \in \mathbb{R}^{p_{l-1}}, \norm{\vv{w}_{l, h}}_2 =1, \norm{\vv{w}_{l, h}}_0 \le w_0, c_{l, h}\in\mathbb{R}, l \in \{1,\dots, L-1\}, h\in \{1, \dots, p_{l}\}\}$. Also, let $\{(Y_i, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\}_{i=1}^n$ denote a sample of size $n$, where each $\boldsymbol{X}_i$ is a $p$-dimensional random vector supported on $[0, 1]^p$, and $Y_i$ is a real-valued random variable (e.g., $Y_i$ may be continuous, discrete, or a mixture of both). Let the boosting functions $F_{s} : \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} \times \mathbb{R}^{p}\mapsto \mathbb{R} $  be defined recursively such that for every $s\in\{1, \dots, M\}$ and every $\vv{x} \in [0, 1]^{p}$, 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{update.1}
		\begin{split}
			F_{s}(g, \vv{x}) &\coloneqq \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}_i) \times  \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = g(\vv{x}) \} }{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = g(\vv{x}) \} },\\
			g_s & = \argmin_{g \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} } \sum_{i=1}^{n} (m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}_i) - F_s(g, \boldsymbol{X}_i))^2, \\
			m_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}_i) &= m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}_i) - \gamma F_{s}(g_s, \boldsymbol{X}_i),    
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	for some constant learning rate $\gamma\in (0, 1]$, where  $m_0(\boldsymbol{X}_i) \coloneqq Y_{i}$. Ties are broken randomly. The output prediction based on \eqref{update.1} at  $\vv{x}\in [0, 1]^p$ of this neural network is given by 
    \begin{equation}
        \label{update.3}
        \widehat{m}_M(\vv{x}) \coloneqq \gamma\sum_{s=1}^{M} F_{s} (g_s, \vv{x}).
    \end{equation}


	% Boosted 01Neuro exhibits strong predictive capabilities. Section~\ref{Sec4.new} presents its key statistical property called universal approximation under the infinite-sample setup. On the other hand, 

    Section~\ref{Sec4.1} demonstrates that our proposed model updating approach, called Exploit-Explore Optimizing, can solve the nonconvex optimization problem in \eqref{update.1} and achieve sure convergence, meaning the optimization reliably converges in a probabilistic sense, given sufficient computational resources. On the other hand, Example~\ref{exmp.1} in Section~\ref{sec3.2} and Table~\ref{tab:validation_sapce_01neuro} in Section~\ref{Sec5.3} offer recommended practical choices for 01Neuro architectures.



	\section{Sure Convergence via Exploit-Explore Optimizing}\label{Sec4.1}

	\subsection{Exploit-Explore Optimizing}\label{sec3.1}

In this section, we introduce the Exploit-Explore Optimizing for solving the nonconvex optimization problem defined in \eqref{update.1}. The method updates a network function iteratively, aiming to eventually reach an optimal configuration. Specifically, consider a generic training sample $\{U_i, \boldsymbol{X}_i\}_{i=1}^n$, a network function $g \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$ (as defined in \eqref{ilw}) with weights and biases $(\vv{w}_{L, 1}, \dots, \vv{w}_{1, p_{1}}, c_{L, 1}, \dots, c_{1, p_{1}})$ initialized to zero, and a corresponding predictive function $F: \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} \times \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}$, defined as
\begin{equation}
\label{eew.opti2}
F(g, \vv{x}) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} U_i \times \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = g(\vv{x}) \}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = g(\vv{x}) \}}.
\end{equation}
In what follows, we describe how Exploit-Explore Optimizing updates the network function $g$ in a single round. As a remark, to solve the optimization problem in \eqref{update.1}, we set $U_i = m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}_i)$ and $F = F_s$ and apply Exploit-Explore Optimizing over multiple update rounds for each $s \in \{1, \dots, M\}$.




 In each round, the neuron units of $g$ are updated sequentially, starting from the output layer and moving toward the input layer. At the $l$th layer, each unit $h \in \{1, \dots, p_l\}$ is updated in order. Specifically, for the $(l, h)$th neuron with $l \in \{1, \dots, L\}$, we update its weight vector and bias $(\vv{w}_{l, h}, c_{l, h})$ as follows.


    \textbf{Sampling Candidate Weights and Biases: } We begin by sampling \(K\) candidate pairs of weight vectors and biases, denoted by \((\vv{u}_1, b_1), \dots, (\vv{u}_K, b_K)\). Each $w_0$-sparse weight vector \(\vv{u}_k\) is sampled uniformly at random from the unit sphere in \(\mathbb{R}^{p_{l-1}}\), i.e., 
	$\vv{u}_k \in_R \left\{ \vv{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{p_{l-1}} : \|\vv{w}\|_2 = 1, \norm{\vv{w}}_0\le w_0 \right\},$
	where \(a \in_R A\) for some finite set \(A\) denotes that \(a\) is drawn uniformly at random from \(A\), and \(K > 0\) is a fixed integer (a hyperparameter). Bias sampling is performed after weight sampling and varies according to the layer index: For layers \(l > 1\), each bias is sampled as \(b_k = \vv{u}_{k}^{\top} \vv{e}\), where \(\vv{e} \in_R \{0, 1\}^{p_{l-1}}\) is a randomly sampled binary vector. For the first layer \(l = 1\), the bias is set as \(b_k = \vv{u}_{k}^{\top} \boldsymbol{X}_i\), where \(i \in_R \{1, \dots, n\}\) is a randomly selected training index. 

	\textbf{Optimizing Weights and Biases: } Among the \(K\) sampled candidate pairs, we choose the pair \((\vv{u}_{k}, b_{k})\) that minimizes the training loss when substituted into the model.
	 Let each output function \( \{g_{1}, \dots, g_{K}\} \subset \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} \) be defined to share the same weights and biases as \( g \), except that \( (\vv{w}_{l,h}, c_{l,h}) \) is replaced by \( (\vv{u}_k, b_k) \) for each \( k \in \{1, \dots, K\} \). Additionally, $g_{K+1} = g$ is included to ensure that the fitted loss does not increase. Now, the current network output function $g$ is updated to new function $g^{\text{new}}$, defined as:
    \begin{equation}
        \label{eew.opti}
        \begin{split}                
    g^{\text{new}}& = \argmin_{g\in\{ g_1, \dots, g_{K+1}\}} \sum_{i=1}^n \left(U_i - F(g, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\right)^2,
    \end{split}
    \end{equation}
where $F$ is given by \eqref{eew.opti2}. Here, ties are broken by selecting the $g_i$ with the higher index.

After all neural units in the model have been updated in a round, the procedure proceeds to the next round of updating.




