In each update round, optimization of the form \eqref{eew.opti} is performed only on active neurons, with their total number bounded by $\sum_{l=1}^L w_0^{L-l}$, where at most $w_0^{L - l}$ neurons are active in the $l$th layer. This holds regardless of the number of idle neurons (see Figure~\ref{fig:sparsen1} and the remark before Condition~\ref{sparsity.1} in Section~\ref{sec3.2} for definitions of active and idle neurons). For idle neurons not connected to the output, $g^{\text{new}}$ is sampled uniformly from $\{g_1, \dots, g_{K+1}\}$ to reduce computational cost. Although idle neurons do not directly affect the output, they still contribute to the training complexity. In particular, Section~\ref{SecB} shows that the training complexity of boosted 01Neuro over a single update round is approximately linear in $nMK \left( \sum_{l=1}^{L} p_l p_{l-1} \right) \left( \sum_{l=1}^L w_0^{L-l} \right)$ when $n$ is large. This result can be used to estimate the training time required for the recommended 01Neuro architectures presented in Table~\ref{tab:validation_sapce_01neuro} of Section~\ref{Sec5.3}.


For comparison, training a single 01Neuro model (without boosting) with $b = 1$ update round requires computational time comparable to that of training a fully grown decision tree~\citep{breiman2001random}, given the 01Neuro architectures listed in Table~\ref{tab:validation_sapce_01neuro}. In our experiments reported in Sections~\ref{Sec6}–\ref{Sec7}, a boosted 01Neuro model with $M \in \{3, 4, \dots, 50\}$, using bagging (see Section~\ref{Sec5}), typically requires 5 to 30 times more computational time than the slower of Random Forests or XGBoost. Detailed runtime statistics for real data experiments are provided in Table~\ref{tab:runtime_comparison} of Section~\ref{Sec7}.




 On the other hand, idle neurons play a critical role in enabling effective optimization, as discussed in Sections~\ref{sec3.1.2}–\ref{sec3.2}. The pyramid-shaped architecture suggested in Example~\ref{exmp.1} strikes a practical balance between active and idle neurons at each layer.






\subsubsection{Optimization Dynamics}\label{sec3.1.2}


Exploit-Explore Optimizing in \eqref{eew.opti} provides effective optimization dynamics and prevents convergence to local optima by enabling continued exploration of alternative network structures through idle neurons. For example, in Figure~\ref{fig:sparsen1}, if all active neurons $f_{2,1}$ and $f_{2,2}$ at the second layer becomes stuck in a suboptimal configuration, an idle neuron such as $f_{2,4}$ can explore other combinations—recall that applying \eqref{eew.opti} to an idle neuron is equivalent to randomly selecting $g^{\text{new}}$ from ${g_1, \dots, g_{K+1}}$. An idle neuron may randomly select a promising subnetwork, becoming active in subsequent rounds as higher layers are updated (e.g., for $f_3$). The sparse architecture defined in Condition~\ref{sparsity.1} ensures such exploration remains possible at every layer, supporting 01Neuro’s sure convergence property, which is formally stated in Theorem~\ref{theorem1}. It is worth noting that having many idle neurons increases the chances of discovering better network structures during optimization. However, as discussed in Section~\ref{sec3.1.1.b}, idle neurons still consume computational resources, even though they are not actively optimized.

	\subsection{Sure Convergence}\label{sec3.2}

	Gradient-based methods are not directly applicable to indicator activation functions because the gradients of such networks are zero almost everywhere~\citep{li2021differentiable}, making optimization particularly challenging due to the absence of informative updates. In contrast, Exploit-Explore Optimizing takes advantage of this zero-gradient property to optimize weights and biases effectively. The key idea is that the indifferent region (the zero-gradient region) surrounding the optimal weights and bias of 01Neuro is sufficiently large that a randomly sampled pair of weights and bias falls within it with positive probability, thereby motivating the design of Exploit-Explore Optimizing. This intuition is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:equivalent_split}, where pairs of weights and biases from the indifferent region separate the five samples into the same two subsamples (black and gray). 

