    Lemma~\ref{lemma10} formally establishes that for any input set $\mathcal{N}$ consisting of elements from $[0, 1]^k$ and any pair $(\vv{w}, c)$, if we sample a $k$-dimensional unit-length vector $\vv{w}'$ uniformly at random (for instance, by sampling $k$ i.i.d. standard Gaussian components and then normalizing), then with positive probability there exists some $\vv{v} \in \mathcal{N}$ such that the pair $(\vv{w}',(\vv{w}')^{\top} \vv{v})$ produces the same subsamples as $(\vv{w}, c)$, as in \eqref{sample-euquivalent}. Here, the input set $\mathcal{N}$ may represent either the original input samples or intermediate representations within the network. The bias sampling strategy in Exploit-Explore Optimizing, introduced in Section~\ref{sec3.1}, together with Lemma~\ref{lemma10}, forms the basis for the sure convergence property of 01Neuro in Theorem~\ref{theorem1} below. 

	% \ref{SecD.1} %%
	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma10}

		Consider a set of input vectors $\mathcal{N}$ consisting of elements from $[0, 1]^k$. There exists some constant $\varepsilon>0$ such that for every pair $(\vv{w}, c)\in \{(\vv{u}, b) : \vv{u} \in\mathbb{R}^k, \norm{\vv{u}}_2= 1, b\in\mathbb{R}\}$, there is a measurable set of  weight vectors $\mathcal{E}(\vv{w}, c)\subset\{\vv{w} \in\mathbb{R}^k: \norm{\vv{w}}_2= 1\}$  with at least positive surface measure $\varepsilon$ on the unit sphere in $\mathbb{R}^k$ such that for every $\vv{w}^{'} \in \mathcal{E}(\vv{w}, c)$, it holds that 
        \begin{equation}
            \label{sample-euquivalent}
            \{\vv{u} \in \mathcal{N}: (\vv{w}^{'})^{\top}\vv{u} - (\vv{w}^{'})^{\top}\vv{v} > 0\} = \{\vv{u} \in \mathcal{N}: \vv{w}^{\top}\vv{u} - c > 0\}
        \end{equation} 
        for some $\vv{v}\in \mathcal{N}$ if $\texttt{\#} \{\vv{u} \in \mathcal{N}: \vv{w}^{\top}\vv{u} - c > 0\} < \texttt{\#}\mathcal{N}$.

	\end{lemma}

The proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma10} is deferred to the online Supplementary Material.
We now formally introduce a key concept in the 01Neuro architecture: active and idle neuron units. For each \( g \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} \) with \( g(\vv{x}) = \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}^{\top}\vv{f}(\vv{x}) > c\} \) for some \( \vv{f} \in \mathcal{F}_{L-1, w_0} \), \( c \in \mathbb{R} \), and a \( w_0 \)-sparse unit-length weight vector \( \vv{u} \in \mathbb{R}^{P_{L-1}} \), the neuron unit \( f_{l,h} \) is said to be active in \( \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}^{\top}\vv{f}(\vv{x}) > c\}  \) if it either directly belongs to the support of \( \vv{u} \) (i.e., $h \in \{j : |u_j| > 0\}$ at layer \( l = L - 1 \)), or it contributes to an active neuron unit through a nonzero weight. A neuron unit is considered idle if it is not active. See Figure~\ref{fig:sparsen1} for a graphical example of active and idle neuron units. The index set of active and idle neuron units of $\boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}^{\top}\vv{f}(\vv{x}) > c\}$ is given by \( \{(l, h) : l \in \{1, \dots, L - 1\},\ h \in \{1, \dots, p_l\}\} \). 


Condition~\ref{sparsity.1} guarantees sufficient idle neuron units for exploring network structures. A practical 01Neuro structure satisfying this condition is presented in Example~\ref{exmp.1} below.  In Theorem~\ref{theorem1}, the initial 01Neuro model is denoted by $g^{(0)} \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$, while $g^{(b)} \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$ denotes the model after $b$ rounds of Exploit-Explore Optimizing.

	\begin{condi}\label{sparsity.1}
		For any \( g_1, g_2 \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0} \), there exist \( \vv{f} \in \mathcal{F}_{L-1, w_0} \) and pairs \( (b_i, \vv{u}_i) \in \mathbb{R}^{p_{L-1}+1} \) with \( \norm{\vv{u}_i}_2 = 1 \) and \( \norm{\vv{u}_i}_0 \le w_0 \) such that \( g_i(\vv{x}) = \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}_i^{\top} \vv{f}(\vv{x}) > b_i\} \) for every $\vv{x}\in\mathbb{R}^p$ and \( i \in \{1, 2\} \), and the index sets of active neuron units in \( \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}_1^{\top} \vv{f}(\vv{x}) > b_1\}  \) and \( \boldsymbol{1}\{\vv{u}_2^{\top} \vv{f}(\vv{x}) > b_2\}  \) are disjoint.
	\end{condi}



    % \ref{SecC.1}%%
	\begin{theorem}[Sure Convergence]
		\label{theorem1}
		Let a sample $\mathcal{X}_n = \{(U_i, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}$ be given, along with a 01Neuro architecture satisfying Condition \ref{sparsity.1}, an initial model $g^{(0)} \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$, and $F$ defined by \eqref{eew.opti2}. Apply Exploit-Explore Optimizing for $b$ rounds, and obtain $g^{(b)} \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$. Then,  $\lim_{b\rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{P}\big(\sum_{i=1}^n \left(U_i - F( g^{(b)}, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\right)^2 = \inf_{g\in\mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}} \sum_{i=1}^n \left(U_i - F(g, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\right)^2 \mid \mathcal{X}_n \big) = 1$.
	\end{theorem}

    The proof of Theorem \ref{theorem1} is deferred to the online Supplementary Material.
Theorem \ref{theorem1} ensures that the probability of achieving complete optimization can be made arbitrarily high, provided sufficient computational resources, measured by the number of update rounds $b$. This theorem establishes the sure convergence property for the update rule in \eqref{eew.opti}, under a general training sample and function $F$. To solve the optimization problem in \eqref{update.1}, Exploit-Explore Optimizing can be applied in two ways: either by updating each boosted 01Neuro predictor over multiple rounds (mentioned in Section~\ref{sec3.1}), or by updating all predictors in sequence within each round and repeating this process for multiple rounds (introduced in Section~\ref{Sec5.0}). When $M$ is finite, the same arguments as in Theorem \ref{theorem1} apply directly (with details omitted for brevity) to show that convergence holds for both approaches, thereby ensuring the sure convergence of the boosted 01Neuro model in \eqref{update.1}.




Example~\ref{exmp.1} below summarizes all hyperparameters for our boosted 01Neuro model and its network architecture. Given the sparsity parameter $w_0$, the maximum number of active neuron units at the $l$th layer is $w_0^{L - l}$ for $l \in \{1, \dots, L - 1\}$. Therefore, if $p_l \ge 2w_0^{L - l}$, indicating that there are at least as many idle neurons as active ones at each layer, then Condition~\ref{sparsity.1} is satisfied. The detailed proof of this result is omitted. Meanwhile, Table~\ref{tab:validation_sapce_01neuro} in Section~\ref{Sec5.3} presents the hyperparameter space of 01Neuro used for model tuning.


\begin{exmp}\label{exmp.1}
A boosted 01Neuro model is governed by the following hyperparameters: the number of sampled candidate sets per optimization step $K$ in \eqref{eew.opti}, number of update rounds $b$, sparsity level $w_0$, and layer-wise neuron widths $p_l \ge 2w_0^{L-l}$ for $l \in \{1, \dots, L - 1\}$ with $p_L = 1$. This 01Neuro architecture satisfies Condition~\ref{sparsity.1}. Additionally, there are  boosting learning rate $\gamma \in (0, 1]$ and the number of boosting iterations $M$.    
\end{exmp}

\subsection{Review of Optimization Convergence in Neural Networks}




Training neural networks typically involves minimizing the empirical $L_2$-loss, as defined in \eqref{update.1}, over a given class of network functions. Among the most widely adopted training algorithms are adaptive gradient methods such as Adam and its variants \citep{kingma2015adam, hinton2012neural, duchi2011adaptive}, which have recently been shown to converge under certain conditions \citep{zhang2022adam, li2023convergence, defossez2020simple}. Nevertheless, both theoretical \citep{jentzen2024non} and empirical \citep{choromanska2015loss} studies have demonstrated that gradient-based optimization tends to converge to local minima. While many local minima in sufficiently large or overparameterized networks yield good generalization performance \citep{choromanska2015loss}, significant challenges remain due to the gap between theoretical guarantees and the practical behavior of neural networks \citep{adcock2021gap, grohs2024proof}.

Complementary to this, a line of work has investigated the convergence and generalization properties of infinitely wide neural networks trained via gradient descent \citep{jacot2018neural, cao2019generalization, arora2019exact}, or adaptive methods such as Adam \citep{malladi2023kernel}, under the framework of the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK). In parallel, \citep{mei2018mean, mei2019mean} offer a mean field perspective on the distributional dynamics of model parameters updated by stochastic gradient descent in overparameterized single-hidden-layer networks, analyzing the conditions under which local or global optima may be attained. While the NTK and mean field approaches have significantly advanced the theoretical understanding of optimization in neural networks, their reliance on assumptions such as infinite or very large network width still limits their direct applicability to practical architectures with moderate width used in real-world applications. 



In summary, achieving reliable and interpretable optimization in neural network training remains a central open problem. The sure convergence property Theorem~\ref{theorem1} contributes a promising step toward addressing this challenge.









	\section{Universal Approximation of Boosted 01Neuro}\label{Sec4.new}



In this section, we establish the universal approximation property of the boosted 01Neuro of \eqref{update.1}. In light of Theorem~\ref{theorem1}, our approximation framework based on \eqref{update.1} is constructive \citep{gentile2024approximation}, or algorithm-dependent \citep{fan2020selective}, in that it accounts for the network’s training procedure. In particular, it draws on principles from greedy approximation theory \citep{temlyakov2000weak} to guide the selection of weak predictors during the boosting process \citep{friedman2001greedy}, with each selected predictor optimized in a probabilistic sense as described in Theorem~\ref{theorem1}.


The universal approximation theory is developed in the population setting, corresponding to the case of an infinite training sample. At the population level, the response variable is modeled as $Y = m(\boldsymbol{X}) + \varepsilon$, where $\boldsymbol{X}$ is a $p$-dimensional input feature vector and $\varepsilon$ is an independent, zero-mean error term. An independent and identically distributed training sample of size $n$ is denoted by $\{(Y_i, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\}_{i=1}^n$. The infinite-sample setting refers to the learning scenario of \eqref{update.1} in which, for $l \in \{0, 1\}$, every $s > 0$, and each $g \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$, the sample moments
$
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}_i) \times \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = l\}$ and $ \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = l\}
$
are replaced by their population counterparts
$
\mathbb{E}\big(m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}) \times \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}) = l\}\big)$ and $\mathbb{P}\big(g(\boldsymbol{X}) = l\big),
$
respectively. Similarly, the update
$
m_s(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}_i) - \gamma F_s(g_s, \boldsymbol{X}_i)$ for  $i\in \{1, \ldots, n\},$
is replaced by
$
m_s(\boldsymbol{X}) = m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X}) - \gamma F_s(g_s, \boldsymbol{X}),
$
and the initial values $m_0(\boldsymbol{X}_i) \coloneqq Y_i$ are replaced by $m_0(\boldsymbol{X}) \coloneqq m(\boldsymbol{X})$. Lemma~\ref{lemma2} below formally derives $\widehat{m}_M(\vv{x})$ of \eqref{update.3} under the infinite-sample setting.



% \ref{SecD.2} %%%%

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma2}

 Under the infinite-sample setting, the boosting estimator in \eqref{update.1} is given by
\begin{equation}
    \label{lemma2.1}
    \widehat{m}_M(\vv{x}) = \gamma \sum_{s=1}^M\sum_{l=0}^{1} \frac{\boldsymbol{1}\{ g_{s}(\vv{x}) = l\} }{\sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}) =l) }} \times \mathbb{E}( \frac{\boldsymbol{1}\{ g_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}) = l\} }{\sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}) =l) }} m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X})),
\end{equation}
where $g_s$ and $m_{s}$ for $s\in\{1, \dots, M\}$ with $m_0(\vv{x}) = m(\vv{x})$ are recursively defined by
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{split}	\label{pursuit.1}
        \mathcal{P}(g_s, m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X})) & \ge t\times  \sup_{g \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}} \mathcal{P}(g, m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X})),\\
			m_{s}(\vv{x}) &= m_{s-1}(\vv{x}) - \gamma \sum_{l=0}^{1} \frac{\boldsymbol{1}\{ g_{s}(\vv{x}) = l\} }{\sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}) =l) }} \times \mathbb{E}( \frac{\boldsymbol{1}\{ g_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}) = l\} }{\sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g_{s}(\boldsymbol{X}) =l) }} m_{s-1}(\boldsymbol{X})).
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	In \eqref{pursuit.1}, $t=1$ and $\mathcal{P}(g, f(\boldsymbol{X})) \coloneqq \sum_{l=0}^{1} [\mathbb{E}( \frac{\boldsymbol{1}\{ g(\boldsymbol{X}) = l\} }{\sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g(\boldsymbol{X}) =l) }} f(\boldsymbol{X}))]^2$ for every measurable $f:\mathbb{R}^{p}\mapsto\mathbb{R}$ and each $g\in\mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}$. 
\end{lemma}


Population-level boosted 01Neuro obtained via \eqref{pursuit.1} with $t < 1$ corresponds to the weak approximation algorithm~\citep{temlyakov2000weak}, which accounts for practical scenarios where ideal approximation is hindered by estimation variance. Although our analysis focuses on the population setting where estimation variance is absent, we still consider $t \in (0, 1]$ in the approximation theory for generality.








	\subsection{Constructive Universal Approximation}\label{ba.1}



    Under the infinite-sample setting as in Lemma~\ref{lemma2}, we investigate how the boosted 01Neuro approximate the underlying data-generating function, denoted by $m(\boldsymbol{X})$. In the same spirit as illustrated by Figure \ref{fig:sparsen1}, a 01Neuro model can approximate any $k$-sparse rectangular indicator function. Consequently, the boosted 01Neuro function class, denoted by $\mathcal{D}$ below, is capable of approximating any finite sum of $k$-sparse measurable functions. This result is formally established in Lemma \ref{universal.lemma.1}.
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{split}\label{ourcase.1}
			\mathcal{D} =&  \{\sum_{i=1}^{q} a_ig_i: g_i(\vv{x}) \in \{ \frac{g(\vv{x})}{ \sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g(\boldsymbol{X}) = 1) }}, \frac{1- g(\vv{x})}{ \sqrt{\mathbb{P}(g(\boldsymbol{X}) = 0) }}\} , g \in \mathcal{I}_{L, w_0}, a_i\in\mathbb{R}, q \in \mathbb{N}\},		
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}
		where $\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of natural numbers. In particular, the proof of Lemma~\ref{universal.lemma.1} shows that \(\mathcal{D}\) contains all step functions defined on \([0, 1]^p\) that depend on \(k\) coordinates in the \(p\)-dimensional ambient space, provided $L \ge 2 + \lceil \log_2 k \rceil$ and $w_{0} \ge 2$. Here, a step function is defined as a linear combinations of finitely many rectangular indicator functions \citep{stein2009real}. Note that the results of Lemma~\ref{universal.lemma.1} hold regardless of the training method used for 01Neuro.



    \begin{condi}\label{dist.1}
        The distribution of \(\boldsymbol{X}\) is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure and admits a probability density function that is bounded from above.
    \end{condi}

    % \ref{SecD.3} %%%%
	\begin{lemma}\label{universal.lemma.1}	
    Assume Condition \ref{dist.1}. If $w_0 \ge 2$ and $L \ge 2 + \lceil \log_2 k \rceil$, then $\mathcal{L}(k, R_{0}) \subset \overline{\mathcal{D}}$ for each integer $R_0>0$, where $\mathcal{L}(k, R_{0})  = \{\sum_{r=1}^{R_0} m^{(r)}: m^{(r)}\in \mathcal{L}(k)\}$, and that
             \begin{equation*}
		\begin{split}			
			\overline{\mathcal{D}} = & \{m : \text{ Every measurable } m:[0, 1]^p\mapsto\mathbb{R} \text{ with } \mathbb{E}(m(\boldsymbol{X}) )^2 < \infty \\
			& \text{ and } \lim_{i\rightarrow\infty} \mathbb{E}(m(\boldsymbol{X}) - f_i(\boldsymbol{X}))^2 = 0 \text{ for some } f_i\in \mathcal{D}\},\\	
			\mathcal{L}(k)  =& \{ m : \text{Every measurable } m : [0, 1]^{p} \mapsto \mathbb{R}\text{ with } \mathbb{E}[m(\boldsymbol{X})]^2<\infty \text{ and } \\
			& \quad \mathbb{E}(|m(\boldsymbol{X}) - m^{\dagger}(X_{i_1}, \dots, X_{i_k})|)=0    \textnormal{ for some  }  
			m^{\dagger} : [0, 1]^{k} \mapsto \mathbb{R} \\
			& \quad \textnormal{ and some }  \{i_{1}, \dots, i_{k}\} \subset \{1, \dots, p\}\}.
		\end{split}
	\end{equation*}
	\end{lemma}



Based on the results of Lemma~\ref{universal.lemma.1}, which highlight the potential of boosted 01Neuro without considering the training procedure, we further show in Theorem~\ref{theorem2} that the population-level boosted 01Neuro defined in \eqref{lemma2.1} is a constructive universal approximator for any function that is a sum of measurable functions, each depending on possibly different sets of $k$ coordinates. 
Here, $R_{0}$ denotes an arbitrary fixed number of additive measurable components in the data-generating function.




    % \ref{SecC.2}%%%
	\begin{theorem}
		\label{theorem2}
Assume Condition~\ref{dist.1}.  Let  $w_0 \ge 2$, $L \ge 2 + \lceil \log_2 k \rceil$, $m\in \mathcal{L}(k, R_{0})$, and $\widehat{m}_{M}(\boldsymbol{X})$ as in \eqref{lemma2.1} be given, with \( t \in (0, 1] \) and \( \gamma \in (0, 1] \). Then $\lim_{M\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}[\widehat{m}_{M}(\boldsymbol{X}) - m(\boldsymbol{X})]^2 = 0$.
	\end{theorem}


	The proof of Theorem \ref{theorem2} is deferred to the online Supplementary Material. The consistency of general boosting methods has been studied by \citep{biau2021optimization}, while the approximation properties of weak greedy algorithms, which are closely related to our analysis of the boosting algorithm in Theorem~\ref{theorem2}, have been investigated by \citep{temlyakov2000weak}. However, Theorem~\ref{theorem2} focuses on their universal approximation capability, a topic not addressed by \citep{biau2021optimization} or \citep{temlyakov2000weak}. More related works to Theorem~\ref{theorem2} are reviewed in Section~\ref{Sec4.2}. 

    The results and arguments for Theorems~\ref{theorem1}--\ref{theorem2} provide a comprehensive understanding of how network depth enhances the approximation capability of 01Neuro through boosting, and how network width, combined with the sparse structure of 01Neuro, facilitates sure convergence in training. Our findings on the role of depth align with existing literature showing that deeper networks can achieve more efficient approximation \citep{eldan2016power, telgarsky2016benefits}.     Note that Theorem~\ref{theorem2} does not quantify the effects of $t < 1$ and $\gamma < 1$ on the approximation error, as convergence rates are not established in this result.


