	\subsection{Discussion}\label{Sec4.2}

    The universal approximation theory for single-hidden-layer neural networks was initially established by \citep{hornik1989multilayer} and \citep{cybenko1989approximation}. Dimension-free approximation convergence rates for such networks were first provided by \citep{barron1993universal}. Extensions of these results to multilayer neural networks have been developed by \citep{bauer2019deep} and \citep{schmidt2020nonparametric}. These theoretical results focus on the expressive power of neural network function classes, without addressing how such functions can be computed in practice, a framework often referred to as algorithm-independent control \citep{fan2020selective, gentile2024approximation}. For a formal discussion of the gap between approximation theory and practical implementation in neural networks, see \citep{adcock2021gap, grohs2024proof}.


    On the other hand, constructive approximation methods, which establish approximation guarantees in conjunction with the training procedure, have been extensively studied using greedy algorithms \citep{herrmann2022constructive, siegel2022optimal, barron2008approximation}. In particular, \citet{barron2008approximation} propose a greedy algorithm that sequentially selects neuron units in a single-hidden-layer network, resulting in a universal approximator. However, their results are limited to shallow architectures and do not extend to deeper networks, which are central to modern deep learning due to their ability to learn hierarchical feature representations. Additionally, \citet{barron2008approximation} require regularization of the function class complexity to apply the greedy algorithm, while the theoretical guarantees assume optimization over the full function class. This suggests that the resulting approximator is not fully constructive.



    In contrast, Theorems~\ref{theorem1}--\ref{theorem2} collectively demonstrate that our boosted 01Neuro acts as a constructive universal approximator. Particularly, 01Neuro provides a practical solution to nonconvex optimization problems, making it well-suited for real-world applications. This is particularly valuable in field studies that require task-specific representation learning, where the underlying optimization problems are typically nonconvex in nature.

