	\section{Stable Training and Bagging for 01Neuro}\label{Sec5}



In the previous section, we introduced the vanilla version of boosted 01Neuro alongside its solid statistical properties. Starting from this section, we shift our focus toward practical training techniques that are designed to perform well in practice. The evaluation of these methods is primarily based on empirical results, as demonstrated by the data experiments in Sections \ref{Sec6}--\ref{Sec7}.

    \subsection{Training a Sequence of Boosted 01Neuros}\label{Sec5.0}

In practice, a single update round for $\widehat{m}_M(\boldsymbol{X})$ (with notation as in \eqref{update.1}--\eqref{update.3}) is performed through the following steps. (1) Given the training sample $\{(Y_i, \boldsymbol{X}_i)\}_{i=1}^n$, we first update the first 01Neuro predictor for a single round and compute the residuals $m_1(\boldsymbol{X}_i)$. (2) Using $\{(m_1(\boldsymbol{X}_i), \boldsymbol{X}_i)\}_{i=1}^n$ as training data, we then update the second 01Neuro predictor for a single round and compute the residuals $m_2(\boldsymbol{X}_i)$. (3) We proceed in a similar manner to update each subsequent 01Neuro predictor until all $M$ predictors have been updated. This training scheme may be repeated for a total of $b$ rounds to produce the final boosted 01Neuro predictor $\widehat{m}_M(\boldsymbol{X})$. Note that at the beginning of each round, the model's initial parameters are set to those obtained at the end of the previous round.


