\subsection{Stochastic and Stabilized Exploit-Explore Optimizing}\label{Sec5.4}

Each optimization step in \eqref{eew.opti} is performed using a randomly selected subset of the training data, comprising a proportion defined by the hyperparameter \textsf{stochastic\_ratio} $\in [0.8, 1]$. That is, $\textsf{stochastic\_ratio} \times \text{(training sample size)}$ examples are randomly sampled without replacement for each optimization. Subsampling helps stabilize optimization in finite-sample settings, similar to how stochastic gradient descent \citep{robbins1951stochastic} improves upon standard gradient descent. Additionally, in \eqref{eew.opti}, we subtract $\textsf{stabilizer} \times n^{-1} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (U_i - \bar{U})^2$, where $\bar{U} = n^{-1} \sum_{i=1}^{n} U_i$ and $\textsf{stabilizer}\in \{0.5, 0.1, 0.01, 0\}$ is a hyperparameter, from the objective associated with the current-stage candidate parameter $g_{K+1}$ to enhance the stability of the optimized network model.



    \subsection{Bagging}\label{Sec5.2}


Let a boosted 01Neuro model $\widehat{m}_{M}(\vv{x})$ be given. Bagging \citep{breiman2001random} of $N_{\text{bagging}}$ weak predictors based on $\widehat{m}_{M}(\vv{x})$ can be performed efficiently as follows. First, independently draw $N_{\text{bagging}}$ bootstrap samples (with replacement) from the training set, each of the same size as the original training data. For each bootstrap sample, initialize a model $\widehat{m}_{M}^{(r)}(\vv{x})$ identical to $\widehat{m}_{M}(\vv{x})$ and fine-tune it (via the approach introduced in Section~\ref{Sec5.0}) for a few additional rounds. The final predictive model makes predictions by averaging over the $N_{\text{bagging}}$ weak predictors, i.e., using $N_{\text{bagging}}^{-1} \sum_{r=1}^{N_{\text{bagging}}} \widehat{m}_{M}^{(r)}(\vv{x})$ at any test point $\vv{x} \in [0, 1]^p$. By default, we set $N_{\text{bagging}} = 20$ and use 10 rounds of fine-tuning. As we will see in Section~\ref{Sec6}, applying bagging to boosted 01Neuro leads to significant improvements in prediction accuracy.

% performs effectively with bagging. % Using no more than 10 fine-tuning rounds for each of the 20 bagging predictors already results in significant improvements in prediction accuracy.







    \subsection{Training Schedule}\label{Sec5.3}

In this section, we summarize the training procedure for the boosted 01Neuro model used in our experiments. Given a dataset for model training, we optimize hyperparameters by uniformly sampling configurations from the space defined in Table~\ref{tab:validation_sapce_01neuro}. In this table, the $k$th architecture for $k \in \{A, \dots, F\}$ corresponds to the layer configuration $(p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4, p_5)$, given by
$(4, 1, \emptyset, \emptyset, \emptyset)$,
$(8, 4, 1, \emptyset, \emptyset)$,
$(16, 8, 4, 1, \emptyset)$,
$(4, 4, 1, \emptyset, \emptyset)$,
$(4, 4, 4, 1, \emptyset)$, and
$(4, 4, 4, 4, 1)$,
where $\emptyset$ denotes the absence of a layer at the corresponding position. Note that while not all network architectures considered here satisfy Condition~\ref{sparsity.1}, they are adequate for the purposes of our data experiments.

For each configuration, we train a single boosted 01Neuro model using the methods described in Sections~\ref{Sec5.0}–\ref{Sec5.4}, and evaluate its performance on a validation set. During validation,  we train the network for $b = 20$ rounds. Then, we apply 10 bagging predictors, each with one fine-tuning round. This optimization procedure is independently repeated $R\in \{30, 100\}$ times, each time splitting the data into 80\% for training and 20\% for validation. Details of the hyperparameter tuning process are provided in Section \ref{SecA.1}. 



After hyperparameter optimization, we retrain the predictive model using the full dataset for $b = 120$ rounds. We then apply bagging to generated 20 bagging boosted 01Neuro models, each undergoing 10 fine-tuning rounds, to form the final predictive model. If model evaluation is needed, as in Section~\ref{Sec7}, a separate test set should be set aside from the dataset prior to hyperparameter tuning, typically by sample splitting. When using a training, validation, and test split, the final predictive model is trained on the combined training and validation sets. To avoid confusion, we refer to this combined set as the full training set.


