\section{Empirical Evaluation of Prediction Capability}\label{Sec6}

In this section, we evaluation the prediction capability of the boosted 01Neuro from Section~\ref{Sec5.3} by simulating datasets from the following two data-generating models.
\begin{align}
    Y &= \sum_{j=1}^{10}X_j + 1.8\times \varepsilon, \label{m1}\\   
    Y &= 2\times X_{1} \times X_{2} + 0.5 \times X_{3} + 0.3 \times\varepsilon, \label{m3}
\end{align}
where $X$ is drawn from the uniform distribution over the hypercube $[-1, 1]^p$, and the independent model error $\varepsilon$ follows the standard normal distribution. In our data experiments, we simulate $n\in \{150, 450\}$ i.i.d. samples from each of Models \eqref{m1}--\eqref{m3}, with $p = 100$. The signal-to-noise ratio in the first case is approximately 1:1, while the variances of $2 \times X_1 \times X_2$, $0.5 \times X_3$, and $0.3 \times \varepsilon$ are in the approximate ratio 5:1:1. With Models \eqref{m1}--\eqref{m3}, we aim to evaluate the predictive models' ability to handle noisy features with noisy errors, numerous additive components, and challenging scenarios such as complex multiplicative interactions. Given the limited sample size, we focus on these settings where model performance does not plateau, enabling a more meaningful comparison of stability and generalization across methods.



The performance of boosted 01Neuro with bagging (referred to as bagging boosted 01Neuro) is evaluated using R$^2$ scores and compared to its counterpart without bagging (single boosted 01Neuro), as well as to two popular tree-based models: XGBoost \citep{chen2016xgboost} and Random Forests \citep{breiman2001random}. These two serve as representative examples among widely used tree-based and deep learning models \citep{mcelfresh2023neural, grinsztajn2022tree}. For each method, hyperparameters are first optimized using an 80\% training and 20\% validation split of the available data. A final predictive model is then trained on the full sample of size $n$. The hyperparameter optimization involves $R = 30$ independent runs, with the corresponding search spaces and other details are provided in Section \ref{Sec5.3} and Section \ref{SecA.1}. Our search spaces are aligned with state-of-the-art setups proposed in \citep{grinsztajn2022tree, mcelfresh2023neural}. The R$^2$ score is computed on an independently generated test set of size 10,000, and is defined as
\begin{equation}
\label{r_square_score}
\text{R}^2 = 1 - \frac{\text{sum of squared residuals}}{\text{sample variance of the responses}}.
\end{equation}
Each experiment is repeated 100 times to ensure stability, and the results of R$^2$ scores are reported in Table~\ref{tab:r_square_example}. Additionally, Figure~\ref{fig:hist} displays the histograms of the optimized hyperparameter values of $M$ and the selected architectures for $n = 450$, aggregated over the 100 trials.


