\textbf{Insights from Table~\ref{tab:r_square_example}: } Let us summarize the advantages of the boosted 01Neuro given results from Table~\ref{tab:r_square_example}. First, the results for Model \eqref{m3} demonstrate the universal approximation ability of boosted 01Neuro, aligning with Theorem~\ref{theorem2}. Our model outperforms other tree-based methods on this model, which includes a multiplicative interaction term often referred to as an XOR component. The XOR structure is a standard benchmark for testing a model’s capacity to learn complex interactions \citep{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}, and it is well known that orthogonal decision trees have difficulty capturing such patterns \citep{hastie2009elements}. Second, the proposed model exhibits low sensitivity to noisy features, as evidenced in Table~\ref{tab:r_square_example}, where the boosted 01Neuro performs comparably to other tree-based models even when $p \approx n$. Third, the results obtained without feature normalization for 01Neuro suggest that normalization is unnecessary. Fourth, Table~\ref{tab:r_square_example} demonstrates that the training techniques introduced in Section~\ref{Sec5}, especially the bagging method from Section~\ref{Sec5.2}, enhance the prediction stability of boosted 01Neuro, making it comparable to the well-established stability of Random Forests. An exception is Model~\eqref{m3} with $n = 450$, where the proposed method occasionally achieves exceptionally high performance, leading to greater variability in R$^2$ scores. 



A potential limitation of the proposed model, not apparent from Table~\ref{tab:r_square_example}, is that boosted 01Neuro has a higher computational cost compared to other tree-based models. As noted in Section~\ref{sec3.1.1.b}, boosted 01Neuro typically requires 5 to 30 times more computational time than Random Forests and XGBoost, revealing opportunities to improve its efficiency. For simplicity, detailed runtime statistics are not reported here for the simulated data experiments. However, comprehensive runtime results based on real data are presented in Section~\ref{Sec7}. The current implementation details of boosted 01Neuro, which does not utilize GPU acceleration, are provided in Section~\ref{sec3.1.1.b}, Section~\ref{Sec9}, and Section~\ref{SecA.2}.


\textbf{Insights from Figure~\ref{fig:hist}: }  For Model \eqref{m3}, which includes a complex XOR component, deeper architectures (specifically architectures C, E, and F, which each contain at least three layers) are necessary for accurate prediction. Because this model does not involve many additive terms, a large value of $M$ is not essential for learning. In contrast, for the linear Model \eqref{m1}, the choice of architecture is more balanced, with overly shallow networks being less favorable. Since Model \eqref{m1} comprises several additive components, increasing $M$ typically improves predictive accuracy. The histograms in Figure~\ref{fig:hist} demonstrate how boosted 01Neuro adapts to different data-generating processes based on model complexity.


