\section{Benchmarking Predictive Accuracy on Real Data}\label{Sec7}



In this section, we highlight the predictive accuracy and stability of the boosted 01Neuro with bagging from Section~\ref{Sec5.3} by comparing it against Random Forests \citep{breiman2001random} and XGBoost \citep{chen2016xgboost}. Specifically, we evaluate performance using 4 selected datasets, namely elevators, house\_16H, yprop\_4\_1, and abalone, from OpenML \citep{OpenML2013} and the UCI Machine Learning Repository \citep{Dua:2017}, based on the R\(^2\) goodness-of-fit measure. We use a dataset of size up to $n_{\text{dataset}} \in \{1000, 5000\}$ for our evaluation. If the dataset contains more than $n_{\text{dataset}}$ samples, we randomly select $n_{\text{dataset}}$ samples; otherwise, we use the entire dataset. Our evaluation approach follows the methodology established in \citep{mcelfresh2023neural, grinsztajn2022tree}, which provides a state-of-the-art benchmark procedure and simulation framework.

To justify our choice of benchmark models, we note that XGBoost and Random Forests consistently demonstrate strong performance across the four tabular regression datasets studied, often outperforming both deep learning models such as ResNet \citep{he2016deep} and SAINT \citep{somepalli2021saint}, as well as other tree-based methods \citep{grinsztajn2022tree}. To avoid redundancy and simplify hyperparameter tuning, we focus on these two representative models. For readers interested in state-of-the-art deep learning methods like TabPFN \citep{hollmann2022tabpfn}, which can also be competitive with tree-based models, we refer to \citep{mcelfresh2023neural}.





