We now highlight the advantages of boosted 01Neuro based on the results in Tables~\ref{tab:r_square_updated_high}--\ref{tab:r_square_updated_high_large}.
First, the overall results demonstrate that the proposed model is competitive in predictive performance relative to the two selected benchmark models. While XGBoost leads on the elevators dataset, boosted 01Neuro achieves the best performance on abalone and ranks among the top on house\_16H and yprop\_4\_1.  Second, these results were obtained without feature normalization, demonstrating the convenient applicability of 01Neuro. Third, due to the stable training techniques introduced in Section~\ref{Sec5}, boosted 01Neuro demonstrates strong performance stability in small- and medium-sized datasets ($\le 2500$ training sample size) compared to XGBoost and Random Forests. It is worth noting that the performance gains of XGBoost on the house\_16H and abalone datasets as sample size increases are primarily due to a reduced incidence of extremely poor results, indicating its potential instability in small-sample scenarios.


We now discuss a potential limitation of the proposed model. The current consensus in tabular prediction performance evaluation \citep{mcelfresh2023neural} suggests that top-tier tree-based and deep learning models offer comparable accuracy across a wide range of tabular applications. However, some models, such as CatBoost \citep{prokhorenkova2018catboost}, which is cited as an example in \citep{mcelfresh2023neural}, are notably easier to use due to requiring minimal hyperparameter tuning and offering efficient training times. As shown in Tables~\ref{tab:r_square_updated_high} to \ref{tab:r_square_updated_high_large}, the proposed model performs comparably to the selected benchmark models on three out of the four evaluated datasets, with the exception of elevators, where XGBoost consistently outperforms the other methods. However, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:runtime_comparison}, Boosted 01Neuro requires approximately 5 to 30 times more training time than the slower of Random Forests and XGBoost, highlighting the need for improvements in algorithmic design or implementation efficiency.







\section{Discussion}
We propose a new deep learning model, 01Neuro, based on indicator activation neurons. Its boosted variant exhibits two key statistical properties:  sure convergence and constructive universal approximation. Unlike most universal approximation results that are independent of training procedures, our guarantees are directly linked to the model’s explicit construction and optimization algorithm.

Three promising directions for future research based on 01Neuro are as follows. First, providing a theoretical characterization of its approximation convergence rate and extending the framework to establish high-dimensional consistency, where the number of features grows with the sample size, would deepen our understanding of the model. Second, developing more efficient optimization algorithms is essential to overcome the current computational bottleneck and make the model practical for large-scale applications. Third, applying 01Neuro to time series data for signal extraction, feature representation, and transfer learning offers a promising direction for future research.


