	\subsection{Training Complexity of 01Neuro}\label{SecB}


We briefly discuss the implementation and computational cost of Exploit-Explore Optimizing introduced in Section \ref{sec3.1} in this section. In light of \eqref{eew.opti2}, the optimization \eqref{eew.opti} can be represented by a maximization problem of \begin{equation}
    \label{tensor.1}
    g^{\text{new}}= \argmax_{g\in \{g_{1},\dots, g_{K+1}\}} \sum_{l=0}^{1} \left(\frac{ \sum_{i=1}^{n} U_i \times  \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = l) \} }{ \sqrt{ \sum_{i=1}^{n} \boldsymbol{1}\{g(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = l) \} } }\right)^2.
\end{equation} 
This optimization problem can be solved by using tensor (i.e., multidimensional array) computing as follows. 

\noindent \textbf{1)} Generate $\{g_{1}, \dots, g_{K+1}\}$ by sampling according to the procedure in \eqref{eew.opti}.


\noindent \textbf{2)} Perform a forward pass to compute the $n\times (K+1) $ binary matrix $M_X$ defined as
\[
M_{X} =\begin{bmatrix}
 g_{1}(\boldsymbol{X}_1)  & \dots & g_{K+1}(\boldsymbol{X}_1)  \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
g_{1}(\boldsymbol{X}_n) & \dots & g_{K+1}(\boldsymbol{X}_n)
\end{bmatrix}.
\]


Let $f_L$ be equivalently denoted by $f_{L,1}$; throughout what follows, the superscript $(k)$ indicates that the weights and biases associated with $g_k$ are being used. In Step 2), for each $k\in \{1, \dots, K+1\}$ and each $h \in \{1, \dots, p_l \}$ at each layer $l\in \{1, \dots, L\}$, we compute the binary outputs $f_{l,h}^{(k)}(\boldsymbol{X}_1), \dots, f_{l,h}^{(k)}(\boldsymbol{X}_n)$ by evaluating $W_{l,h}^{(k)} \odot M_{X,l-1}^{(k)} > C_{l,h}^{(k)}$, where $\odot$ means element-wise product, $M_{X,l-1}^{(k)} = (\vv{f}_{l-1}^{(k)}(\boldsymbol{X}_1), \dots, \vv{f}_{l-1}^{(k)}(\boldsymbol{X}_n))^{\top} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p_{l-1}}$ is the previous layer’s output matrix, $W_{l,h}^{(k)} = (\vv{w}_{l,h}^{(k)}, \dots, \vv{w}_{l,h}^{(k)})^{\top} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p_{l-1}}$ repeats the weight vector, and $C_{l,h}^{(k)} = (c_{l,h}^{(k)}, \dots, c_{l,h}^{(k)}) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ repeats the threshold. The evaluation can be broadcasted across all $(h, k)$ without explicit iteration. This computation leverages element-wise products and broadcasting for efficiency. 

\noindent \textbf{3)} Compute the column-wise sum of $M_X$ to obtain vector $A_1$, and set $A_0 = (n, \dots, n) - A_1$.

\noindent \textbf{4)} Compute $S_1 = (U_1, \dots, U_n) \times M_X$, and set $S_0 = \left(\sum_{i=1}^n U_i, \dots, \sum_{i=1}^n U_i\right) - S_1$.

\noindent \textbf{5)} Complete the optimization in \eqref{tensor.1} by computing
$
\frac{S_l(k)}{\sqrt{A_l(k)}} = \frac{ \sum_{i=1}^n U_i \times \boldsymbol{1}\{g_k(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = l\} }{ \sqrt{ \sum_{i=1}^n \boldsymbol{1}\{g_k(\boldsymbol{X}_i) = l\} } }
$ for each index $k\in \{1, \dots, K+1\}$ and $l \in \{0, 1\}$,
 where $S_l(k)$ and $A_l(k)$ denote the $k$th elements of $S_l$ and $A_l$, respectively. These computations are vectorized using $S_0, S_1, A_0, A_1$. 


\begin{remark}
Tensor computing involves operations on multi-dimensional arrays (tensors), encompassing linear algebra tasks such as matrix multiplication, element-wise operations, and broadcasting. Broadcasting specifically refers to the automatic expansion of array dimensions to enable arithmetic operations between tensors of different shapes.
\end{remark}


The number of basic operations such as additions, multiplications, and comparisons required to complete a single optimization of \eqref{tensor.1} is approximately linear in $nK \left( \sum_{l=1}^{L} p_l p_{l-1} \right)$ when $n$ is large, as detailed below. The cost of sampling in Step 1 above and broadcasting operations is ignored in this calculation. Step 2) involves evaluating each neuron $h \in \{1, \dots, p_l\}$ at layer $l\in \{1, \dots, L\}$ for each $k\in \{1, \dots, K+1\}$, and it takes $p_{l-1}n$ multiplications and $n$ comparisons per neuron. Summing over all layers, this results in a total of
$
(K+1)\sum_{l=1}^{L} p_l(p_{l-1}n + n)
$
basic operations. Step 3) requires $n(K+1)$ additions to compute the column-wise sums. Subtractions are treated as additions in the operation count. Step 4) involves $n(K+1)$ multiplications and an additional $n(K+1)$ additions. Step 5) requires $2(K+1)$ square root evaluations, $2(K+1)$ divisions, and $K+1$ comparisons to compute the final $\arg\max$. 

Combining all contributions, the total number of basic operations in a single optimization of \eqref{tensor.1} is
$ 3n(K+1) + 6(K+1) + (K+1)\sum_{l=1}^{L} p_l(p_{l-1}n + n)$. This total is linearly proportional to $nK (\sum_{l=1}^{L} p_lp_{l-1})$ when $n$ is large, as the remaining terms are of lower order. Additionally, as discussed in Section~\ref{sec3.1.1.b}, there are at most $\sum_{l=1}^L w_0^{L-l}$ active neuron units to be updated in each round. The overall operations required in one round of updating is linearly proportional to $\sum_{l=1}^L w_0^{L-l} \times nK (\sum_{l=1}^{L} p_lp_{l-1})$. As a result, the training complexity of the boosted 01Neuro over a single round of update is $M\sum_{l=1}^L w_0^{L-l} \times nK (\sum_{l=1}^{L} p_lp_{l-1})$.

