\begin{abstract}
For a directed graph $G$, let $\mathrm{mindeg}(G)$ be the minimum 
among in-degrees and out-degrees of all vertices of $G$. 
It is easy to see that $G$ contains a directed cycle of length at least $\mathrm{mindeg}(G)+1$.
In this note, we show that, even if $G$ is $2$-connected, it is NP-hard to check
if $G$ contains a cycle of length at least $\mathrm{mindeg}(G)+3$. 
This is in contrast with recent algorithmic 
results of Fomin, Golovach, Sagunov, and Simonov [SODA 2022] for
analogous questions in undirected graphs. 
\end{abstract}

