\subsection{Approach A: Explanation through language}
\label{subsec:verbal}
In this approach, the custom GPT model was tasked with identifying and segmenting a series of complex motion sequences using only the kinematic characteristics defined in advance in the model configuration. Importantly, in this approach, the model was not provided with any examples, training data, or data retrieved from motion primitives. Instead, is relied entirely on language-based reasoning, using descriptive rules about each motion behavior (see \autoref{tab:motion_characteristics}).  
% To guide this approach, the prompt provided to the GPT consisted of a structured natural language query and was specifically tailored to identify motions and segment complex motions into their constituent motion types based on distinctive kinematic characteristics. 
The prompt was aimed at looking at velocity profiles for the purpose of classifying motion boundaries, order of occurrence, and segmentation indices. 
% Additional constraints, defined outside the model’s configuration, ensured non-overlapping, non-repetitive motion instances within each sequence, with a variable number of constituent motions per sample.


\subsection{Approach B: Providing example measurements}
\label{subsec:examples}
Unlike the language-based reasoning of Approach A, this method, introduces an intermediate stage of learning where the GPT is provided with representative motion examples, corresponding to the individual motion primitives: \textit{pull}, \textit{swing}, \textit{slide}, \textit{tilt}, \textit{twist}. In this approach, the specified kinematic characteristics of each motion are not provided to the GPT. Instead, the model has to learn and understand these characteristics implicitly. 
% This method is meant to enhance the model's understanding of motion primitives by allowing it to learn an internal representation for every motion type through example-based reasoning.
% Based on patterns in translational and rotational behavior, the model was told to analyze these signals, produce visualizations, and identify the distinctive features of each primitive. 
% These initial exploratory tasks were used to ground the GPT’s understanding of each class of motion. Once the motion primitives were introduced and analyzed, the model was then provided with complex motion sequences for motion identification and segmentation. At this stage, it was required to compare the new input with the previously acquired primitives, while the remainder of the prompt remained unchanged from Approach A.


\subsection{Approach C: Combining explanations with examples}
\label{subsec:both}
Approach C integrates the language-based reasoning of Approach A with the example-based reasoning of the Approach B, aiming to leverage the strengths that emerge from the combination of these two approaches. In more details, the model here is provided with both the specific kinematic characteristics and the representative motion examples. 
%This particular approach allows the model to cross-reference descriptive rules with patterns observed in data, improving this way the motion recognition and the segmentation accuracy of the complex motion sequences. 

The performance of the approaches in 20 different complex motion sequences (involving 56 primitive actions) are provided in Table \ref{table:ClassificationResults} and Fig. \ref{fig:box} for the classification and segmentation respectively. Notice that all 20 sequences are considered as the testing dataset for Approaches A, B and C, without the requirement of a ``validation'' dataset.

