\begin{abstract}
%For over six decades, the Judd-Ofelt (JO) theory has been a fundamental framework in rare-earth optical spectroscopy, describing intra $4f\leftrightarrow4f$ transitions through three phenomenological intensity parameters. Despite its widespread use, a standardized computational methodology for precise and reproducible JO parameter determaination remains limited. In this study, we present LOMS.cz (Luminescence, optical and magneto-optical software), an interactive web-based computational tool that enables automated JO analysis, including the computation of transition probabilities, branching ratios, and theoretical radiative lifetimes with or without included magnetic-dipole corrections. LOMS integrates a dynamically expanding database of experimentally validated JO parameters, allowing direct comparisons between computed and empirical data. Additionally, the newly implemented Combinatorial JO (C-JO) analysis algorithm systematically identifies the most critical absorption band combinations, ensuring the reliability and consistency of calculated spectroscopic parameters. This approach combines computational modeling, data-driven methodologies, and high-throughput screening to enhance the predictive capabilities of materials characterization, facilitating the development of advanced photonic and optoelectronic materials through \textit{in silico} design and optimization.

We present LOMS.cz (\textbf{L}uminescence, \textbf{O}ptical and \textbf{M}agneto-optical \textbf{S}oftware), an open-source computational platform that addresses the long-standing challenge of standardizing Judd-Ofelt (JO) calculations in rare-earth spectroscopy. Despite JO theory's six-decade history as the fundamental framework for understanding $4f\leftrightarrow4f$ transitions, the field lacks standardized computational methodologies for precise and reproducible parameter determination. LOMS integrates three key innovations: (1) automated computation of JO parameters, transition probabilities, branching ratios, and theoretical radiative lifetimes, (2) a dynamically expanding database of experimentally validated parameters enabling direct comparison between computed and empirical results, and (3) a novel Combinatorial JO (C-JO) analysis algorithm that systematically identifies optimal absorption band combinations to ensure reliable parameter extraction. As a proof-of-concept, we demonstrate how this computational framework enables rapid screening of spectroscopic parameters, allowing researchers to predict optical properties with enhanced reliability. By combining automated analysis with experimental validation through its integrated database, LOMS.cz establishes a standardized platform for accelerating the discovery and optimization of rare-earth-based photonic and optoelectronic materials.

\end{abstract}
