\section{Related Work}
\subsection{3D Mesh Generation}
With the success of 2D generation \cite{2ddiff}, 3D generation has emerged as a popular research direction. Initially, SDS-based optimization methods \cite{d1,d2,d3,d5,d4,d6} were employed to address the scarcity of 3D datasets, followed by subsequent works \cite{dd1,dd2} aimed at resolving the Janus problem. As 3D datasets have expanded \cite{objaverse,objxl}, feed-forward 3D generation methods \cite{f1,f2,f3} have seen significant advancements. While these approaches greatly enhance generation speed, they suffer from low-quality meshes and limited diversity. More recent methods \cite{v1,v3,v4,v5,v6,v7,v8,v9,v10,v2} focus on learning compressed 3D representations via VAEs combined with latent diffusion models. However, above methods typically rely on voxels or SDFs to represent 3d shape, and then translate object to dense triangle meshes using algorithms like Marching Cubes \cite{mc}. This process frequently results in subpar geometric topology, characterized by poorly structured or cluttered wireframes.

\subsection{Auto-Regressive Based Mesh Generation}

To generate meshes with high quality topology, recent methods leverage autoregressive models to learn vertex distributions and connections directly from handcrafted meshes. MeshGPT \cite{siddiqui2024meshgpt} pioneered this approach by encoding meshes into tokens using VQ-VAE\cite{vq}, enabling direct supervision from handcrafted data.
MeshXL \cite{chen2024meshxl} identified that quantizing triangle vertex tokens without trainable VQ-VAE is key to scaling datasets. Following this, MeshAnything v2 \cite{chen2024meshanythingv2} and EdgeRunner \cite{tang2024edgerunner} introduced compact tokenization algorithms, increasing the maximum face count from 800 \cite{chen2024meshanything} to 1.6k and 4k, respectively. BPT \cite{weng2024scaling} and TreeMeshGPT \cite{lionar2025treemeshgpt} further optimized both geomotry compression and vertex compression supporting 5k-8k faces.
For autoregressive model optimization, Meshtron \cite{hao2024meshtron} proposed an hourglass transformer \cite{hourglass} and truncation training, enabling up to 16k face generation. LLaMAMesh \cite{wang2024llama} leveraged pretrained LLMs for text-to-mesh generation, while DeepMesh \cite{zhao2025deepmesh} applied reinforcement learning via Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) \cite{dpo}, significantly improving mesh aesthetics.


\section{Method}

% Our mesh tokenization algorithm consists of the following steps: (1) Preprocessing: We developed a lightweight non-manifold edges processing algorithm to ensure that the input meshes strictly adhere to manifold topology requirements. (2) Vertex Layering and Sorting: Starting from a given half-edge, all vertices are classified into multiple layers resembling contour lines, and are subsequently sorted based on their local order. (3) Layer Adjacency Matrices Compression: Two layer adjacency matrices are computed for each layer and then compressed into tokens. (4) Token Packing: Vertex tokens, along with their corresponding topology tokens (compressed from layer adjacency matrices), are organized into a unified format optimized for auto-regressive generation.
Our mesh tokenization algorithm consists of the following steps: 
(1) Preprocessing: We developed a lightweight, non-manifold edge processing algorithm to ensure that the input meshes strictly adhere to manifold topology requirements. 
(2) Vertex Layering and Sorting: Starting from a given half-edge, all vertices are classified into multiple layers resembling contour lines and are subsequently sorted based on their local order. 
(3) Layer Adjacency Matrices Compression: Two-layer adjacency matrices are computed for each layer and then compressed into tokens. 
(4) Token Packing: Vertex tokens and their corresponding topology tokens (compressed from layer adjacency matrices) are organized into a unified format optimized for an auto-regressive generation.

\subsection{Non-manifold Edges Processing}

\begin{figure*}[t] % 'r' 表示图片在右侧，宽度为 0.5\textwidth
% \vspace{-15pt}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{./images/pipe/pipe_nonmani_v3.pdf} % 调整图片宽度
    \caption{Illustration of non-manifold edge processing. Naive non-manifold edge splitting will result (b) or (c) since triangles associated with non-manifold edges are indeterminate, while the latter compromised the integrity of surface. Our algorithm keeps the integrity of local surface.}
    \label{fig:pipe-nonmani}
     \vspace{-5pt}
\end{figure*}
The non-manifold edges for a mesh refer to the edges shared by 3 or more faces. As illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:pipe-nonmani}, the edge 9-10 is non-manifold edge since it is shared by triangle 9-10-11, 9-10-8 and 9-4-10. A naive way to eliminate it, as done in EdgeRunner \cite{kang2019decoupling}, is to re-traverse all of the triangle edges and mark the third-occurring edge edge as bounding edge. However, this may cause several results depending on the order in which the triangles appear. As shown in (b) and (c) of Fig. \ref{fig:pipe-nonmani}, though both of the results indicate the non-manifold edges are eliminated, the latter destroyed the completeness of mesh surface which is not friendly for our following steps.
To solve this, we transform this problem into an equivalent edge graph partition question. In our defination, the vertex $i$ 's edge graph $\mathcal{G}_i$ indicates an undirected graph formed by all of the faces including the vertex. As shown in (d) of Fig. \ref{fig:pipe-nonmani}, the "nodes" in edge graph $\mathcal{G}_9$ represent the edges connected to reference vertex 9 in mesh, while the "edges" in edge graph represent the faces in mesh, which are formed by the "nodes" and reference vertex 9. To guarantee manifold structure for mesh, the edge graph of each mesh vertex should be either a cycle or a chain without any branch, as well as having only one connected component. The whole algorithm is illustrated in (d)-(g) of Fig. \ref{fig:pipe-nonmani}, after checking $\mathcal{G}_9$, the branched "edge" 10-11 (indicating the face 9-10-11 of mesh) is easily detected and will be detached from the cycle. Then at (e), we exclude the isolated connected component 10-11 from edge graph, which is equivalent to detach face 9-10-11 from vertex 9 in mesh, and register a new vertex 12 for mesh edge 10-11. After (e), notice the edge graph of $\mathcal{G}_{10}$ will also be updated. Then (f) will start for checking $\mathcal{G}_{10}$ and so on.  The algorithm proceeds along all of the non-manifold points  with BFS traversal and updates edge graph for each vertex dynamically. More complex details are presented in Appendix A.

\subsection{Vertexes Layering and Sorting}
\label{sec3.2}
\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.35\textwidth} % 'r' 表示图片在右侧，宽度为 0.5\textwidth
\vspace{-15pt}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.35\textwidth]{./images/pipe/pipe_sorting_v2.pdf} % 调整图片宽度
    \caption{The counterclockwise order of neighbor vertices for vertex $a$ is easily obtained via half-edge data structure. Hence the next layer vertices' order are decided.}
    \label{fig:pipe-sorting}
    \vspace{-20pt}
\end{wrapfigure}
As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:pipe-main}, given a manifold mesh $\mathcal{M}$ and a start half-edge, all vertices $\mathcal{V}$ can be assigned a unique coordinate $(L,i)$, and we denote the vertex as $\mathcal{V}^L_i$. The two vertices of start half-edge are marked as $\mathcal{V}^0_1$, $\mathcal{V}^1_1$. The $L$, denoted as layer number, represents the shortest path to the initial vertex $\mathcal{V}^0_1$ alone the edges of $\mathcal{M}$, which is easily obtained by BFS traversal. The $i$, denoted as the layer order, represents the order of the vertices in the same layer $L$, which can be obtained based on the local order of vertices in layer $L-1$. Take Fig. \ref{fig:pipe-sorting} as an example, for vertex a of layer $L$, the neighbor vertices firstly can be arranged counterclockwise based on half-edge data structure, hence the 3 vertices' order (g, h, b) for layer $L+1$ is decided. Further, given all of the sorted vertices of layer $L$, the order of all vertices in layer $L+1$ can be decided in such a manner. Notice the start half edge $\mathcal{V}^0_1$-$\mathcal{V}^1_1$ of mesh is given, the sequence of the sorted vertices on each layer can be determined one by one. In practice, we sort all of the half-edges of given mesh in the order of $y$-$z$-$x$ and select the smallest one as start half-edge.

