\section{Experiments}


\subsection{Dataset and Metrics}
\textbf{Datasets.} 
The model is trained on the mixture of gObjaverse \cite{zuo2024gobj1,qiu2024gobj2} (a subset of Objaverse \cite{objaverse} with \textasciitilde 280k meshes), ShapeNetV2 \cite{chang2015shapenet}, 3D-FUTURE \cite{3dfu20213d} and Toys4K \cite{toys4k} with around 100K meshes without manual selection. 
The meshes with token length > 10000 and max vertex number per layer > m (m=200) are filtered.
Following TreeMeshGPT \cite{lionar2025treemeshgpt}, 1000 meshes of gObjaverse and 200 meshes of other datasets are sampled and reserved for evaluation, with the remainder used for training.

\textbf{Metrics. } We apply four metrics to evaluate the point cloud conditioned generation quality, including Chamfer Distance (CD), Hausdorff Distance (HD), Normal Consistency (NC and |NC|). CD and HD are geometric metrics used to evaluate the similarity between point clouds or meshes. 
% CD captures overall matching by averaging bidirectional nearest neighbor distances, while HD focuses on the worst-case discrepancy by measuring the maximum distance. 
Normal Consistency (NC) assesses the alignment of surface normals, with higher values indicating better consistency. Its absolute form, |NC|, removes directional polarity, focusing purely on overall normal consistency.
In addition, we defined a new metric FR named face number ratio to evaluate the new face generation ability of auto-regressive model, which is calculated by:
$
\text{FR}=f_{\text{pred}}/f_{\text{gt}}
$, 
where $f_{\text{pred}}$ is the number of faces of generated mesh and $f_{\text{gt}}$ is the face number of ground truth mesh.

\subsection{Implementation Details}
The decoder-only transformer has 24 layers and a hidden size of 1024, and the trainable parameter number is around 500M. 
It is trained on 16 $\times$ H800 with around 15 days. 
The AdamW~\cite{adaw} is used as optimizer with $\beta_1$=0.9, $\beta_2$=0.99 and the learning rate starts from $1e^{-4}$ and decreases to $5e^{-5}$ based on cosine curve. 
More details are presented in the Appendix.


