\section{Introduction}
\subsection{State of art}
One of the most fundamental results in harmonic analysis is identifying function spaces that remain invariant under Fourier-type transforms. The Schwartz space, for example, is defined as the set of smooth functions and their derivatives that decay rapidly at infinity. Introduced by Laurent Schwartz in the 1940s, the Schwartz space was pivotal in the development of distribution theory and continues to play a crucial role in modern mathematical analysis. A key property of Schwartz spaces is their invariance under the classical Fourier transform, which makes them the natural domain for tempered distributions.


{Although many Fourier‐transform extensions, such as the Hankel transform \cite{Tr2001Book} and the Dunkl transform \cite{Jeu93, ChTr, Tr02}, fit neatly into the harmonic analysis setting of the classical Schwartz space, this compatibility breaks down for certain Fourier‐type transforms. Under these transforms, the standard Schwartz space is no longer invariant.}

These observations naturally lead to the introduction of adapted Schwartz-type spaces, specifically designed to capture the structural and analytical features of such transforms.
For instance, the traditional Schwartz space does not capture the necessary decay and regularity for the Jacobi transform (see \cite{BeDa}), the Chebli-Trimèche transform (see \cite{Tr81}) among others (see also \cite{De}). These issues lead to modifications, such as the inclusion of an exponential term.


More recently, Gorbachev, Ivanov, and Tikhonov showed in \cite{GoIvTi23} that the classical Schwartz space is not compatible with the $(k,a)-$generalized Fourier transform, $\mathcal{F}_{k,a}$, highlighting that 
the identity $\mathcal{F}_{k,a}\left(\mathcal{S}(\R^N)\right)=\mathcal{S}(\R^N)$ is valid only if $a=2$.  
They analyze this issue in detail in \cite[Section 5]{GoIvTi23}, presenting a counterexample consisting of the Gaussian functions $e^{-\| x\|^2}$.  This refutes \cite[Lemma 2.12]{Jo} (see also \cite[Remark 5.3]{GoIvTi23}) in the case $a\neq 2$.
Moreover, the condition $\displaystyle \frac{2}{a}=n\in \N$, which is common in the one-dimensional case (cf.~\cite{BNS}), is necessary to ensure that $\mathcal{F}_{k,a}(\mathcal{S}(\R))$ contains functions that decay rapidly at infinity
(cf.~\cite[Proposition 5.2]{GoIvTi23}).

In our paper, we construct a Schwartz-type space adapted to the one-dimensional $\kn-$generalized Fourier transform $\mathcal{F}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$.  We show that this space is invariant under the action of $\mathcal{F}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$ and establish a density theorem in weighted $L^p-$spaces, for all $1 \leq p < \infty$. 
These results establish a framework for the systematic study of function spaces associated with the $\kn-$generalized Fourier transform.

In this work, we use the radial deformation of the variable \( x \), which is defined as
\(
x_{\frac{1}{n}} := \sqrt{n}|x|^{\frac{1}{n}} \operatorname{sgn}(x).
\)
A prototypical example is the family of Gaussian functions
\(
e^{-sn |x|^{\frac{2}{n}}},
\) with \( s > 0 \). The invariance property of these functions is naturally deduced from their underlying spectral structure, as encapsulated by \begin{eqnarray}
		\label{Expkn}\left(\mathcal{F}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}e^{-sn\left| \cdot\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}}\right)(x)= \displaystyle \displaystyle \frac{1}{(2s)^{kn-\frac{n}{2}+1}}e^{-\frac{n\left| x\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}}{4s}}, \quad x\in \R.
	\end{eqnarray}


Ben Sa\"id, Kobayashi and \O rsted employed the equality  \eqref{Expkn} in \cite[Theorem 5.29]{BKO} to establish a Heisenberg-type uncertainty principle for the $(k,a)-$generalized Fourier transform. More recently, in \cite{JiNe} the second author collaborated with Jilani to provide a comparative analysis between a function and its $\kn-$generalized Fourier transform via \eqref{Expkn}. This analysis was subsequently used to extend the so-called Hardy and Cowling-Price theorems. These developments underscore the necessity of introducing a novel Schwartz-type space comprising the family of exponential functions $\left(e^{-sn\left|\cdot\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}}\right)_{s>0}$, which are invariant under the action of $\mathcal{F}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$.


\subsection{Our Approach}


Before presenting our main contributions, we establish the foundational concepts necessary for understanding the problem.

 Here and elsewhere we use the notation
\begin{eqnarray*}
	\displaystyle \N=\{n\in \Z~:~n\geq 1\}, &\N_0=\N\cup \{0\}, & \R_+=\{x\in \R~:~x\geq 0\},
\end{eqnarray*}
and $k:=\langle k \rangle$ for the index underlying to the reflection group $\Z_2\cong\{-1,1\}$ of $\R$. 
Considering the following $(k,a)-$deformation of the Hermite operator $\displaystyle \Delta-\|x\|^2$, induced by the Laguerre type operator
\begin{eqnarray*}
	\Delta_{k,a}:=\| x\|^{2-a}\Delta_{k}-\| x\|^a,&a>0,
\end{eqnarray*}
Ben Sa\"id, Kobayashi and \O rsted introduced in \cite{BKO} the so-called $(k,a)-${\it generalized Fourier transform} as follows:
\begin{eqnarray}	\label{FkaRN}	\mathcal{F}_{k,a}=\exp\left(\frac{i\pi}{2a}\left(~2\langle k \rangle+N+a-2~ \right)\right)\exp\left(\frac{i\pi}{2a}\Delta_{k,a}\right),\end{eqnarray} where $\Delta_{k}$ is the so-called Dunkl Laplacian 
studied by Dunkl \cite{Du89,Du91,Du92}, de Jeu \cite{Jeu93,Jeu06}, R\"osler \cite{Ro98,Ro99,Ro03,Ro03-book}, Trim\`eche \cite{Tr01,Tr02}, among others.

In the one-dimensional setting, the symbol \(\Delta_k\) stands for a differential-difference operator induced by the abelian group \(\mathbb{Z}_2\). The following component-wise action defines it:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{DunklLaplacian}
	(\Delta_{k} f)(x)=f''(x)+\frac{2k}{x}f'(x)-k\frac{f(x)-f(-x)}{x^2}, & x \in \R \setminus \{0\}.
\end{eqnarray}

For the sake of readability, we will use the shorthand notation  $\mathcal{F}_{k,n}$ instead of $\mathcal{F}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$ throughout the paper, where $\displaystyle a=\frac{2}{n}$.

Recall that the Schwartz space $\mathcal{S}(\R)$ is defined by the requirement that every iterated application of the first-order operators $\displaystyle X = xI$ and $\displaystyle D = \frac{d}{dx}$, where $I$ denotes the identity operator, satisfies the seminorm condition
\begin{eqnarray} \label{sup-Schwartz} \sup_{x\in \R} \left|~X^\alpha D^\beta f(x) ~\right|<\infty, & \text{for all} & \alpha,\beta \in \N_0. \end{eqnarray}

These operators generate the three-dimensional Weyl-Heisenberg algebra, which encapsulates the essential symmetries of bosonic particles. However, the standard Weyl-Heisenberg correspondence does not apply in this setting due to the absence of a $\kn-$generalized Dunkl operator, $T_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$, that satisfies the condition $\left(T_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\right)^2=\left|x\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}$ for $\frac{2}{n}\neq 2$. Consequently, it becomes necessary to extend the scope beyond $\mathcal{S}(\R)$ by considering the following $\kn$-extension:

\begin{definition}\label{knSchwartz}
	We define $\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ as the space of all {$f\in C^\infty(\R\setminus\{0\})$}
	such that 
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{sup-kn}
		\sup_{x\in \R\setminus\{0\}}~\left|~\left(\left|x\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{\alpha} \left(\left|x\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}\right)^\beta \left(x^\ell f^{(\ell)}(x)\right)~\right|<\infty, &\mbox{for all}& \alpha,\beta,\ell \in \N_0.
	\end{eqnarray}
\end{definition}

In other words, to define $\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ we replaced in \eqref{sup-Schwartz} the operators $X$ and $D$ by $\left|x\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}I$ and $\left|x\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}$, respectively, and instead of considering a function $f\in C^\infty(\R)$, we worked with the sequence of functions $\displaystyle \left(X^\ell D^\ell f\right)_{\ell\in \N_0}$.

The core results of this paper correspond to three key theorems, whose proofs appear in Section \ref{knSchwartz-section} and Section~\ref{Lp-section}. 
In Subsection \ref{knSchwartz-recursive} we will characterize the space $\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ in terms of the generators of the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{sl}(2,\R)$, realized via the differential-difference operators on $\R\setminus\{0\}$ (cf.~\cite[Theorem 3.2]{BKO}): 
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{LadderOp}
	\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+=\dfrac{in}{2}\left|x\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}, & \mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-=\dfrac{in}{2}\left|x\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k,&
	\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}=nx\frac{d}{dx}+\left(kn+1-\frac{n}{2}\right)I.
\end{eqnarray} 

In Subsection \ref{ProofTheorem1}, we prove the following main theorem:

\begin{thm}\label{Fkn-Schwartz-Theorem}
	For the $\kn-$generalized Fourier transform $\mathcal{F}_{k,n}$, one has  $$\mathcal{F}_{k,n}\left(\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)\right)=\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R).$$
\end{thm} 

The proof of Theorem \ref{Fkn-Schwartz-Theorem} is derived from the combination of the results obtained in Subsection \ref{knSchwartz-recursive} with the one-dimensional version of the intertwining properties established in \cite[Theorem 5.6]{BKO} on a dense subpace of $L^2(d\mu_{k,n})$:
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{knFourierIntertwining}
	\begin{array}{lll}
		\displaystyle \mathcal{F}_{k,n}\circ x \frac{d}{dx}=-\left(x\dfrac{d}{dx}+\left(2k+\frac{2}{n}-1\right)\right) \circ \mathcal{F}_{k,n},\\
		\displaystyle \mathcal{F}_{k,n}\circ \mid x \mid^\frac{2}{n}I=-\mid x \mid^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k \circ \mathcal{F}_{k,n},
		\\
		\displaystyle \mathcal{F}_{k,n} \circ \mid x \mid^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k= -\mid x \mid^\frac{2}{n}I~\circ \mathcal{F}_{k,n}.
	\end{array}
\end{eqnarray}

Another feature of the \(\kn-\)generalized Fourier transform is the density of {$\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$} in \(L^p-\)spaces with weighted measure $d\mu_{k, n}$, given by
\begin{equation}
	\label{knMeasure}
	d\mu_{k ,n}(x)=c_{k ,n}|x|^{2k +\frac{2}{n}-2}dx, \quad\text{where}\quad c_{k ,n}=\frac{1}{2\Gamma(k  n-\frac{n}{2}+1)}\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)^{k  n-\frac{n}{2}}.\end{equation} 


For the sake of readability, we will also use the shorthand notation $L^p(d\mu_{k,n})$ ($1\leq p\leq \infty$) when referring to the weighted Lebesgue spaces $L^p(\R,d\mu_{k,n})$ over $\R$. The norm that endows these spaces is given by
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{LpNorm}\|f\|_{L^p(d\mu_{k, n})}=
\begin{cases}
	\displaystyle \left(~\int_\R \vert f(x)\vert^p~d\mu_{k ,n}(x)~\right)^{\frac{1}{p}}&,~1\leq p<\infty \\ \ \\
	\displaystyle \inf\left\{~\lambda>0~:~\left(d_{k,n}f\right)(\lambda)=0~\right\} &,~p=\infty,
\end{cases} 
\end{eqnarray}
where
\begin{eqnarray*}	\left(d_{k,n}f\right)(\lambda):=\mu_{k,n}\left(~\left\{~x\in\R~:~\,|f(x)|>\lambda~\right\}~\right), & \lambda >0.
\end{eqnarray*}

If $f$ is a $C^{\infty}-$function, then it turns out that $\displaystyle \|f\|_{L^\infty(d\mu_{k, n})}=\sup_{x\in \R} |f(x)|$.

In the classical setting, one employs the subspace \(\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})\) of \(\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})\) -- namely, the space of \(C^\infty-\)functions with compact support -- to establish the density of \(\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})\) in \(L^p(\mathbb{R})\) for \(1 \leq p < \infty\). This naturally motivates the introduction of {the} corresponding subset within \(\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)\):

\begin{definition}\label{Dkn-definition}
	We introduce the space $\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R)$ of $C^\infty-$functions on $\R\setminus\{0\}$ with bounded support,  satisfying 
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\sup_{\substack{x\in \R\setminus\{0\} }}~\left|\displaystyle\left(~\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}~\right)^\beta\left(x^\ell f^{(\ell)}(x)\right) \right|<\infty,&\mbox{for all}& \beta,\ell\in\N_0.
	\end{eqnarray*}
\end{definition}


In Subsection \ref{ProofTheorem2}, we proof of the following theorem, which shows that \(\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R)\) is a faithful extension of \(\mathcal{D}(\R)\). Furthermore, we generalize the classical embedding
\(
\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R}) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}),
\)
by extending it to the spaces \(\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\mathbb{R})\) and \(\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\mathbb{R})\), respectively.


\begin{thm}\label{Dkn-thm}
We have the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item[\bf (i)] $\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R)$ is a subspace of $\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$.
	\item[\bf (ii)] The embedding
$\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R)\hookrightarrow \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ is continuous.
\end{itemize}
\end{thm}


In Section \ref{Lp-section}, we extend the embedding and density results for the spaces $\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ and $L^p(d\mu_{k,n})$. {Specifically, the following theorem extend some well-known results from the classical Schwartz space.}

\begin{thm}\label{DensityThm} Let $1\leq p<\infty$. Then the following statements hold:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[\bf (i)] The embedding
		$\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)\hookrightarrow L^p(d\mu_{k,n})$ is continuous.
		\item[\bf (ii)] 	$\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ is a dense subspace in $L^p(d\mu_{k,n})$.
	\end{itemize}
\end{thm}

In Subsection~\ref{ProofTheorem3}, we prove Theorem~\ref{DensityThm} by using Theorem~\ref{Dkn-thm} and the main results outlined in Subsection~\ref{key-subsection}. Specifically, we derive the density of \(\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)\) in \(L^p(d\mu_{k,n})\) from the continuous embeddings
\[
\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R) \hookrightarrow L^p(d\mu_{k,n}),
\]
along with the density of \(\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R)\) in \(L^p(d\mu_{k,n})\). The latter density result is based on the {\it approximation-of-identity theorem} established by the second author in collaboration with Ben Sa\"id in \cite{BeNe2}.

In summary, Theorem~\ref{Fkn-Schwartz-Theorem} demonstrates that the mapping
\[
\mathcal{F}_{k,n}: \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R) \longrightarrow \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)
\]
acts as a topological automorphism on the space of test functions. Moreover, Theorem~\ref{Dkn-thm} and Theorem~\ref{DensityThm} enable the extension of classical results concerning Schwartz functions to a broader function space, which includes \(L^p(d\mu_{k,n})\). Consequently, every function in \(L^p(d\mu_{k,n})\) can be approximated by an element of \(\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)\), thereby allowing the effective application of Fourier transform techniques in this more general setting.

In Section \ref{ConclusionOpen} we propose a conjecture to encourage further investigation of the problem in higher dimensions.

\section{Preliminaries}



\subsection{The $\kn-$generalized kernel $B_{k,n}$}\label{Bkn-section}

We recall that the Schwartz kernel theorem guarantees that the $(k,a)-$generalized Fourier transform, defined in \eqref{FkaRN}, has an integral representation with a continuous, symmetric kernel \(B_{k,a}\) that satisfies the initial condition
\[
B_{k,a}(0,y)=1\quad \text{for all } y\in\mathbb{R},
\]
(see, e.g., \cite[Theorem 5.2.1]{Hormander03}). The boundedness of this kernel depends on both the choice of the parameter \(a\) and the dimension of the underlying space, as discussed in \cite{GoIvTi23, Jo, deBieLM25}. In particular, both de Jeu \cite[Lemma 2.1]{Jo} and Gorbachev et al. \cite[Proposition 2.1]{GoIvTi23} demonstrate that, under the assumption \( a=\frac{2}{n}\) together with \( kn -\frac{n}{2}>-\frac{1}{2}\), the $(k,a)-$generalized Dunkl kernel is uniformly bounded. In the one-dimensional setting, this uniform boundedness is expressed by the condition
\begin{equation}
	\label{Bcondition}
	\forall\, x,y\in\mathbb{R},\qquad \left|B_{k,n}(x,y)\right|\le M,
\end{equation}
for some constant \(0 < M < \infty\), where the notation $B_{k,n}$ stands for $\displaystyle B_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$.

This uniform bound allows us to define the \(\kn-\)generalized Fourier transform for functions in \(L^1(d\mu_{k,n})\) by
\begin{equation}
	\label{FkaRN2}
	(\mathcal{F}_{k,n}f)(y)=\int_{\mathbb{R}} f(x)\, B_{k,n}(x,y)\, d\mu_{k,n}(x), \quad y\in\mathbb{R},
\end{equation}
and to establish the corresponding boundedness estimate
\begin{equation}
	\label{LinftyL1}
	\| \mathcal{F}_{k,n}f\|_{L^\infty(d\mu_{k,n})}\le M\, \|f\|_{L^1(d\mu_{k,n})}.
\end{equation}

Furthermore, the inversion formula (cf. \cite[Theorem 5.3]{BKO})
\begin{equation}
	\label{FourierInversion}
	(\mathcal{F}_{k,n}^{-1}f)(x)= (\mathcal{F}_{k,n}f)\left(( -1)^n x\right),\quad x\in\mathbb{R},
\end{equation}
enables the reconstruction of the original function from its transform. That is,
\begin{equation}
	\label{FourierInversionIntegral}
	f(x) = \int_{\mathbb{R}} (\mathcal{F}_{k,n}f)(y)\, B_{k,n}\left((-1)^n x, y\right)\, d\mu_{k,n}(y).
\end{equation}

The \(\kn-\)generalized Dunkl kernel \(B_{k,n}\) is derived from the eigenfunction property (see, for instance, \cite[Theorem 5.7]{BKO}):
\begin{equation}
	\label{JointEigenfunction}
	|x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}} \, (\Delta_{k}B_{k,n})(x,y) = -|y|^{\frac{2}{n}} \, B_{k,n}(x,y), \quad x,y \in \mathbb{R}.
\end{equation}

This kernel admits a representation in terms of the normalized Bessel function of the first kind of order \(\nu\), denoted by \(\j_{\nu}\). The normalized Bessel function of order \(\nu\) is defined as follows:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{normalizedBessel}
\j_{\nu}(z) = 2^{\nu}\Gamma(\nu+1)z^{-\nu} J_{\nu}(z)
= \Gamma(\nu+1) \sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^m}{m! \, \Gamma(\nu+m+1)}\left(\frac{z}{2}\right)^{2m},
\end{eqnarray}
where \(\Gamma\) denotes the Gamma function and \(J_{\alpha}\) the classical Bessel function of order \(\nu\) (cf.~\cite[Chapter 1]{Tr2001Book}).

Moreover, the kernel \(B_{k,n}(x,y)\) can be decomposed into its even and odd components as follows:
\begin{equation}
	\label{Bkn}
	B_{k,n}(x,y) = B_{k,n}^{e}(x,y) + B_{k,n}^{o}(x,y),
\end{equation}
with the even part given by
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{EvenBkn}
B_{k,n}^{e}(x,y) = \j_{kn-\frac{n}{2}}\Bigl(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}\Bigr),
\end{eqnarray}
and the odd part defined as
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{OddBkn}
B_{k,n}^{o}(x,y) = (-i)^n \left(\frac{n}{2}\right)^n \frac{\Gamma\Bigl(kn-\frac{n}{2}+1\Bigr)}{\Gamma\Bigl(kn+\frac{n}{2}+1\Bigr)} \, xy \, \j_{kn+\frac{n}{2}}\Bigl(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}\Bigr).
\end{eqnarray}


More generally, the iterated application of the operators 
\(n|x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k\) and \(x\dfrac{d}{dx}\)
allows us to express, for every $\alpha,\ell\in \N_0$, the kernel functions
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{BknIterated}
\left( n\, |x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k \right)^\alpha \left( x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell B_{k,n}(x, y), & x,y\in \R
\end{eqnarray}
in the form of an ansatz involving a finite linear combination of {normalized Bessel functions}~\eqref{normalizedBessel}. This result is formalized in the following lemma:
{\begin{lemma}\label{lem}
		We assert that the kernel function defined by \eqref{BknIterated} %=P(|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}},|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})$$ 
		is a linear combination of elements of the type  $\left(\left(|x|^{\frac{1}{n}}\right)^r\left(|y|^{\frac{1}{n}}\right)^q\j_{\nu+s}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right)_{r,q,s\in\N_0}$ %and $\left(\left(|x|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^r\left(|y|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^qxy~\j_{\nu+s}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right)_{r,q,s\in\N_0}$, 
		where $\j_{\nu+s}$ is the normalized Bessel function of index $\nu+s>-\frac{1}{2}$ (see \eqref{normalizedBessel}).
\end{lemma}}






\begin{proof}
	First, we consider the case $\ell=0$. In this case, the assertion follows immediately from \eqref{Bkn}, the joint eigenfunction property \eqref{JointEigenfunction}, and the identity $$xy= \operatorname{sgn}(xy)\left(|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}\right)^n.$$


	Now, assume that $\ell\geq 1$. We begin by recalling the standard Bessel derivative formula for \eqref{normalizedBessel} (see, e.g., \cite[p.~3]{Tr2001Book}):
\begin{equation}\label{Dj}
	\frac{d}{dx}\j_\nu(x) = -\frac{x}{2(\nu+1)}\j_{\nu+1}(x).
\end{equation}
An immediate consequence is that
	$$\left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right) \j_{\nu} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})=-\frac{n}{2(\nu+1)}|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}}\j_{\nu+1}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}). $$

	For a given $\ell\in\mathbb{N}$, suppose that the following induction hypothesis holds:
	 $$\left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell \j_{\nu} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})=\sum_{j=1}^\ell c_{j,\ell}(\nu)~|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}\j_{\nu+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}).$$ 


	 Applying the operator $\displaystyle x\frac{d}{dx}$ to both sides of the previous formula yields

	$\displaystyle \left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^{\ell+1} \j_{\nu} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})=$ $$\begin{array}{ll}
		&\displaystyle =\sum_{j=1}^\ell c_{j,\ell}(\nu)\left(\frac{2j}{n}|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}\j_{\nu+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})-\frac{n}{2(\nu+j+1)}~|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}(j+1)}\j_{\nu+j+1} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right).\\ & \displaystyle =\sum_{j=2}^\ell \left(\frac{2j}{n}c_{j,\ell}(\nu)-\frac{n}{2(\nu+j)}c_{j-1,\ell}(\nu)\right)~|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}\j_{\nu+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})+\\ & \displaystyle \quad+~ c_{1,\ell}(\nu)\frac{2}{n} |xy|^{\frac{2}{n}}\j_{\nu+1} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})+  c_{\ell,\ell}(\nu)\frac{-n}{2(\nu+\ell+1)} |xy|^{\frac{2}{n}(\ell+1)}\j_{\nu+\ell+1} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})                                                                                      
		\\ & \displaystyle = \sum_{j=1}^{\ell+1} c_{j,\ell+1}(\nu)~|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}\j_{\nu+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}).                                                                   \end{array}$$

Reorganizing the sums and reindexing where necessary leads to
	\begin{equation}
		\label{lj}
		\left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell B_{k,n}^{e}(x,y)=\sum_{j=1}^\ell c_{j,\ell}|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}\j_{kn-\frac{n}{2}+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}).
	\end{equation}

Here, $B_{k,n}^{e}(x,y)$ denotes the even part of the kernel $B_{k,n}(x,y)$ (see \eqref{EvenBkn}).~Thus, there exist coefficients $c_{j,\ell}(\nu)$ such that
	$$\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
		\displaystyle c_{j,\ell}(\nu)=\frac{2k}{n}c_{j,\ell-1}(\nu)-\frac{n}{2(kn-\frac{n}{2}+j)} c_{j-1,\ell-1}(\nu); & 2\leq j\leq \ell-1;\\ \displaystyle 
		c_{1,\ell+1}(\nu)=\frac{2}{n}c_{1,\ell}(\nu)\;\; \text{and}\quad c_{1,1}(\nu)=-\frac{n}{2(kn-\frac{n}{2})} \\
		\displaystyle c_{\ell,\ell}(\nu)=-\frac{n}{2(kn-\frac{n}{2}+\ell)}c_{\ell-1,\ell-1}(\nu)                                                                                                                                        \end{array}\right.$$


Next, we focus on the odd part of the kernel, $B_{k,n}(x,y)$, given by \eqref{OddBkn}. By reapplying \eqref{Dj}, we obtain
	$$\left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)\left(xy~ \j_{\nu+n} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right)=xy~ \j_{\nu+n} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})-\frac{n}{2(\nu+n+1)}|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}}xy~\j_{\nu+n+1} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}).$$

An induction argument then shows that for $\nu = kn-\frac{n}{2}$ we have
	\begin{equation}\label{lxj}
		\left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell~\left( xy ~\j_{kn+\frac{n}{2}} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right)=\sum_{j=0}^\ell d_{j,\ell}(\nu)|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}~xy~\j_{kn+\frac{n}{2}+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}),
	\end{equation}
	where the coefficients $d_{j,\ell}(\nu)$ are determined by the following recursive formulas: $$\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
		d_{j,\ell}(\nu)=(\frac{2k}{n}+1)~d_{j,\ell-1}(\nu)-\dfrac{n}{2(kn+\frac{n}{2}+j)} d_{j-1,\ell-1}(\nu)\\
		d_{0,\ell}(\nu)=d_{0,\ell-1}(\nu)=d_{0,0}(\nu)=1\\
		d_{\ell,\ell}(\nu)=-\dfrac{n}{2(kn+\frac{n}{2}+\ell)}~d_{\ell-1,\ell-1}(\nu)   .                                                                                                                                                                  \end{array}\right.$$

	We now proceed by applying the second operator, namely, $n|x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k$. For all $\alpha,\beta\in\N_0$ and any $\nu>-\frac{1}{2}$, we verify that
	\begin{eqnarray} \label{Deltaj}
		\left(n|x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k\right)\left((|x|^\frac{2}{n})^\alpha (|y|^\frac{2}{n})^\beta\j_{\nu}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right)~ =\\ \nonumber (n|y|^\frac{2}{n})^\beta\times 
		\left\{\left(\frac{2\alpha}{n}\left(\frac{2\alpha}{n}-1\right) ~+~\frac{2\alpha}{n}.2k\right)(|x|^\frac{2}{n})^{\alpha-1} \j_{\nu} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right. \\ \nonumber \left.~+~\left(\frac{-nk}{\nu+1}-\frac{4\alpha+2}{2(\nu+1)}\right)(|x|^\frac{2}{n})^{\alpha}|y|^\frac{2}{n}\j_{\nu+1} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right.\\ \nonumber \left.~+~\frac{n^2}{4(\nu+1)(\nu+2)}(|x|^\frac{2}{n})^{\alpha+1}(|y|^\frac{2}{n})^2\j_{\nu+2} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right\}  .
	\end{eqnarray}

{On the other hand,}
$$ (|x|^\frac{2}{n})^\alpha (|y|^\frac{2}{n})^\beta xy~\j_{\nu}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})= \operatorname{sgn}(xy)|x|^{\frac{2\alpha+n}{n}}|y|^{\frac{2\beta+n}{n}}\j_{\nu}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}),$$ 
		which is a consequence of the identity $xy= \operatorname{sgn}(xy)\left(|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}\right)^n$.

	Finally, from \eqref{Dj} and the corresponding property of the Dunkl Laplacian (see \eqref{DunklLaplacian}), one may show that for all $\alpha,\beta\in\N_0$,
	\begin{eqnarray} \label{Deltaxyj}
		\left(n|x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k\right)\left((|x|^\frac{2}{n})^\alpha (|y|^\frac{2}{n})^\beta xy~\j_{\nu}(n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right)~ =\\ %\nonumber n\times 
		=\left\{\left(2k+\frac{(2\alpha+n)}{n}\left(\frac{2\alpha}{n}\right) ~+~\frac{2\alpha+n}{n}.2k\right)(|x|^\frac{2}{n})^{\alpha-1}  (|y|^\frac{2}{n})^\beta xy~\j_{\nu} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right. \nonumber \\ \nonumber \left.~-~\left(\frac{nk}{\nu+1}+\frac{4\alpha+n+2}{2(\nu+1)}\right)(|x|^\frac{2}{n})^{\alpha}|y|^\frac{2\beta+2}{n}xy~\j_{\nu+1} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right.\\ \nonumber \left.~+~\frac{n^2}{4(\nu+1)(\nu+2)}(|x|^\frac{2}{n})^{\alpha+1}(|y|^\frac{2}{n})^{\beta+2}~xy~\j_{\nu+2} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})\right\} .
	\end{eqnarray} 



	Combining the relations \eqref{Bkn}, \eqref{lj}, \eqref{lxj}, \eqref{Deltaj}, and \eqref{Deltaxyj} leads to the desired conclusion of Lemma~\ref{lem}.
\end{proof}


%Hence, for $$a_{k,n}=\left(\frac{in}{2}\right)^n\frac{\Gamma(kn-\frac{n}{2}+1)}{\Gamma(kn+\frac{n}{2}+1)},$$ %we get \\ $\displaystyle \left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell B_{k,n}((-1)^nx,y)=$ $$\sum_{j=1}^\ell c_{j,\ell}|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}\j_{kn-\frac{n}{2}+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}}) +a_{k,n}\sum_{j=0}^\ell d_{j,\ell}|xy|^{\frac{2}{n}j}~xy~\j_{kn+\frac{n}{2}+j} (n|xy|^{\frac{1}{n}})$$

\subsection{Translation and convolution operators for $\mathcal{F}_{k,n}$}\label{Convolution-section}In accordance with \cite[Theorem 4.2]{BNS} and under the condition
\(
kn - \frac{n}{2} > -\frac{1}{2},
\)~
the product formula for two \(\kn\)-generalized Dunkl kernels may be expressed as
	\begin{equation}\label{kernel}
		B_{k,n}(x,w)B_{k,n}(y,w) = \int_\mathbb{R} B_{k,n}(z,w) \,d\nu_{x,y}^{k,n}(z),
	\end{equation}
	where the measure \(d\nu_{x,y}^{k,n}(z)\) is defined by
	\begin{equation}\label{Kkernel} d\nu_{x,y}^{k,n}(z)=\begin{cases}
			\mathcal{K}_{k,n}(x,y,z)d\mu_{k,n}(z) &,~\textrm{if}~xy\neq 0 \\
			d\delta_x(z) &,~\textrm{if}~y=0 \\
			d\delta_y(z) &,~\textrm{if}~x=0.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation} 

	Here, \(\mathcal{K}_{k,n}(x,y,z)\) denotes a non-positive kernel whose support is contained in the set
	$$I_{x,y}=\left\{z\in \mathbb{R}~:~\; \left||x|^\frac{1}{n}-|y|^\frac{1}{n}\right|<|z|^\frac{1}{n}<|x|^\frac{1}{n}+|y|^\frac{1}{n}\right\}.$$

In a natural way, the generalized translation operator, $\tau^{k,n}_{x}$ is then introduced by its integral representation for suitable functions:
	\begin{equation}\label{translation}(\tau^{k,n}_{x}f)(y)=\int_{\mathbb{R}}f(z)\,d\nu_{x,y}^{k,n}(z),\quad x\in\mathbb{R}.
	\end{equation}

For all $\:x,\:y,\:z\in \mathbb{R}$, we immediately have
	\begin{equation}
		(\tau^{k,n}_{x}B_{k,n})(y,z)=B_{k,n}(x,z)\,B_{k,n}(y,z),
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
		\mathcal{F}_{k,n}(\tau^{k,n}_{x}f)(y)= B_{k,n}((-1)^{n} x,y)\mathcal{F}_{k,n}f(y),\end{equation}

		The definition of the generalized translation operator \(\tau^{k,n}_{x}\), as presented in \eqref{translation}, extends naturally to the space \(L^p(d\mu_{k,n})\). In particular, for every \(x \in \mathbb{R}\) and for all \(1 \leqslant p \leqslant \infty\), the following bound holds:
			\begin{equation}\label{normtrans}
			\|\tau^{k,n}_{x} f\|_{L^{p}(d\mu_{k,n})}\leqslant A_{k,n}\| f\|_{L^{p}(d\mu_{k,n})}, 
		\end{equation}
		where the constant \(A_{k,n}\) satisfies \(0 < A_{k,n} < \infty\) and depends solely on the parameters \(k\) and \(n\).

The \(\kn\)-generalized convolution product of two appropriate functions \(f\) and \(g\) is defined via the generalized translation operator as
\begin{eqnarray}\label{convolutionIntegral}
	(f\star_{k,n} g)(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}} f(y) \, \left(\tau_{x}^{k,n} g\right)\Big((–1)^n y\Big)\, d\mu_{k,n}(y), \quad x\in \mathbb{R}.
\end{eqnarray}

{This convolution operation seamlessly integrates with the \(\kn-\)generalized Fourier transform defined in \eqref{FkaRN2}. It}
may be rewritten as
\begin{equation}\label{ConvolutionFormula}
	\left(f\star_{k,n} g\right)(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}} \big(\mathcal{F}_{k,n} f\big)(y) \, \big(\mathcal{F}_{k,n} g\big)(y) \, B_{k,n}\Big((–1)^n x,y\Big) \, d\mu_{k,n}(y), \quad x\in \mathbb{R}.
\end{equation}

The convolution structure satisfies Young's inequality. 
 Specifically, for functions \(f\in L^{p}(d\mu_{k,n})\) and \(g\in L^{r}(d\mu_{k,n})\) with \(1\leq p,q,r\leq \infty\) and satisfying
\(\displaystyle
\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{r}=\frac{1}{q}+1,
\)
the following estimate holds:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{YoungIneq}
	\|f\star_{k,n} g\|_{L^{q}(d\mu_{k,n})} \leq A_{k,n}\,\|f\|_{L^{p}(d\mu_{k,n})} \, \|g\|_{L^{r}(d\mu_{k,n})},
\end{eqnarray}
where \(A_{k,n}\) is a positive constant depending only on \(k\) and \(n\) (see \cite[Properties 5.6]{BNS}).

In particular, if \(1\leq p,q,r\leq 2\) are such that
\(\displaystyle 
\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{r}=\frac{1}{q}+1,
\)
and if \(f\in L^p(\mu_{k,n})\) and \(g\in L^r(\mu_{k,n})\), then the generalized Fourier transform obeys

\begin{equation}\label{FourierknConvolution}
	\mathcal{F}_{k,n}\big(f\star_{k,n} g\big) = \big(\mathcal{F}_{k,n} f\big) \cdot \big(\mathcal{F}_{k,n} g\big).
\end{equation}


\section{The spaces $\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ and $\mathcal{D}_{k,n}(\R)$}
\label{knSchwartz-section}

\subsection{Technical results}\label{knSchwartz-recursive}
Our proof of Theorem \ref{Fkn-Schwartz-Theorem} is based on several facts from Lie algebraic symmetries and combinatorics. First, we establish the following recursive formula, analogous to the one presented in~\cite[Lemma 2.8]{BKO}.
\begin{lemma}\label{RecursiveLemma} For every $f\in C^\infty(\R\setminus \{0\})$ and $\beta \in \N_0$, we have
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k\left( \left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta f(x)\right)&=&4\beta\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{\beta-1}\left((\beta-1)f(x)+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}f(x)\right) \\
		&+&\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k f(x)\right),~~~x\in \R.
	\end{eqnarray*}
	%n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k\left( \left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta f(x)\right)-\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k f(x)\right)
\end{lemma}



\begin{proof}
Recall that the ladder operators $\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+,\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-$ and $\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$ -- defined in \eqref{LadderOp} -- satisfy the commutation relations 
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{sl2} \left[\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+,\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-\right]=\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}, & \left[\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}},\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+\right]=2\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+, & \left[\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}},\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-\right]=-2\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-.
	\end{eqnarray}

In particular, due to $\left[\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}},\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+\right]=2\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+$ it follows that the operator $n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}I=-2i\mathbb{E}_{k, \frac{2}{n}}$ intertwines $\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$ and $2I+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$, i.e.
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}f(x)\right)=n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\left(2f(x)+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}f(x)\right), & x\in \R \setminus \{0\}.
	\end{eqnarray*}

An inductive argument then shows that for each $j\in \N_0$,
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{intertwiningH_beta}
		\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\left(\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^j f(x)\right)=\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^j\left((2j) f(x)+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}f(x)\right), &x\in \R \setminus \{0\}.
	\end{eqnarray}

On the other hand, the commutation relation $\left[\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+,\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-\right]=\mathbb{H}_{k,n}$ implies that
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{commutatorH_E}\left[~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k,n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}I~\right]=\left[-2i\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^-,-2i\mathbb{E}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}^+\right]=4\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}.
	\end{eqnarray}

By combining this with \eqref{intertwiningH_beta} and the Lie bracket identity
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\left[~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k,\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta I~\right]=\\
		=\sum_{j=0}^{\beta-1}\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{\beta-1-j}I~\left[~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k,n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}I~\right]\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^j I
	\end{eqnarray*}
	we deduce that
	\begin{align*}\label{Recursive1}
		\left[~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k,\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta I~\right]f(x)=\nonumber \\
		=4\beta\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{\beta-1}\left((\beta-1)f(x)+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}f(x)\right),~~~x\in \R.
	\end{align*}

	Since the Lie bracket is defined by
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\left[~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k,\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta I~\right]f(x)= \\
		=~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k\left( \left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta f(x)\right)-\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^\beta\left(n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_k f(x)\right),~~~x\in \R,
	\end{eqnarray*} {this completes the proof of} Lemma \ref{RecursiveLemma}.
\end{proof}
%\textcolor{red}{We need the following combinatorial lemma for $\ldots$}


The subsequent lemma and proposition elucidate the relationship between Stirling numbers of the first and second kind and the mapping properties arising from the iterated powers of the operator \(\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\) defined in \eqref{LadderOp}. We establish this connection via a combinatorial argument that employs the boson normal ordering technique introduced by Katriel in \cite{Katriel74}.


\begin{lemma}\label{Schwartz-Lemma}
Let $\left(P_{\alpha,\beta}\right)_{\alpha,\beta\in \N_0}$ denote the family of seminorms
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{seminormP}
	P_{\alpha,\beta}(g)=\sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}} \Bigl| \Bigl( n|x|^{\frac{2}{n}} \Bigr)^\alpha \Bigl( n|x|^{2-\frac{2}{n}} \Delta_k \Bigr)^\beta g(x) \Bigr|,
\end{eqnarray}
and set $(f_m)_{m\in \N_0}$ as the sequence of functions defined by the formula : \begin{eqnarray}
		\label{fm}f_m(x)
		=\sum_{\ell=0}^m \sum_{j=0}^{\ell} \binom{m}{\ell}\left(kn+1-\frac{n}{2}\right)^{m-\ell} n^\ell S(\ell,j)~ x^jf^{(j)}(x), & x\in \R,
	\end{eqnarray}
	where $S(\ell,j)$ denotes the Stirling numbers of the second kind (cf~\cite[subsection 24.1.4]{AS86}).

	Then, we have the following:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[{\bf (a)}] $f\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ if and only if 
			\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{sup-fm}
			P_{\alpha,\beta}(f_m)<\infty, &\mbox{for all}& \alpha,\beta,m \in \N_0.	\end{eqnarray}

	%	\item[{\bf (a)}] If $f\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ then $f_m\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$, holds for all $m\in \N_0$.
		\item[{\bf (b)}] The iterated powers of the operator $\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$ satisfy the mapping property 
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{fm-Mapping}	\left(\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\right)^m:f\mapsto f_{m}, &m\in \mathbb{N}_0.
		\end{eqnarray}
	\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let 
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{X-D}
	X = xI \quad\text{and}\quad D = \frac{d}{dx}
\end{eqnarray}
	denote the multiplication and differentiation operators, respectively.

By Definition~\ref{knSchwartz} and \eqref{seminormP} it easily follows that a function \(f\) belongs to \(\mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\mathbb{R})\) if and only if
	\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{supXjDj}	P_{\alpha,\beta}(X^j D^j f) < \infty, & \text{for all}& \alpha,\beta,j \in \N_0.
	\end{eqnarray}

	Also, from \eqref{fm} the inequality
\[ P_{\alpha,\beta}(f_m) \leq \sum_{\ell=0}^{m} \sum_{j=0}^{\ell} \binom{m}{\ell}  \Bigl(kn + 1 - \frac{n}{2}\Bigr)^{m-\ell} n^{\ell}S(\ell,j)\, P_{\alpha,\beta}(X^j D^j f) \] follows directly from the triangle inequality.

This, together with the inequality~{\( P_{\alpha,\beta}(X^m D^m f) \leq P_{\alpha,\beta}(f_m) \)} immediately shows that verifying the condition \eqref{supXjDj} for \(f\) is equivalent to showing that the condition \eqref{sup-fm} is satisfied. This concludes the proof of statement \textbf{(a)}.

For the proof of {\bf (b)}, we begin by observing that the ladder operators $X$ and $D$ defined by \eqref{X-D} are canonical generators of the boson algebra -- i.e, the Weyl-Heisenberg algebra of dimension $3$ -- so that the {\it boson normal ordering approach} can be used to formally represent the iterated powers $\displaystyle \left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell$, $\ell \in \N$, of the operator $\displaystyle x\frac{d}{dx}=XD$ as follows~(cf.~\cite{Katriel74}): 
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{NormalOrdering}
	\left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell f(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{\ell} S(\ell,j)~ X^jD^jf(x), & x\in \R.
\end{eqnarray}

Here, $S(\ell,j)$ denotes the Stirling numbers of the second kind (cf.~\cite[subsection 24.1.4]{AS86}). So, using the fact that $S(\ell,\ell)=1$ and $S(\ell,j)=0$ for $\ell<j$,
\eqref{fm} admits the binomial representation
\begin{eqnarray}
	\label{fm-binomial}
	f_m(x)=\sum_{\ell=0}^m \binom{m}{\ell}\left(kn+1-\frac{n}{2}\right)^{m-\ell} n^\ell \left(x\frac{d}{dx}\right)^\ell f(x),& x\in \R.
\end{eqnarray}

By noting that the operators  $\displaystyle \left(kn+1-\frac{n}{2}\right)I$ and $\displaystyle nx\frac{d}{dx}$ commute, we prove that $\left(\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\right)^mf(x)$ coincides with \eqref{fm-binomial} for any $m\in \N_0$. This establishes the mapping property \eqref{fm-Mapping}, as desired.
\end{proof}
\begin{proposition}\label{Schwartz-Proposition}
For each $\beta\in \N_0$, let $\left(\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}\right)_{\ell \leq \beta}$ be the sequence of functions defined by
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{fl-Stirling}
		\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}(x)=(-1)^\ell \sum_{j=0}^\ell \sum_{m=0}^j s(\ell,j)\binom{j}{m}\beta^{j-m}f_m(x) &,~x\in \R,
	\end{eqnarray}
	where $(f_m)_{m\in \N_0}$ denotes the sequence of functions defined by~\eqref{fm}, and $s(\ell,j)$ denotes the signed Stirling numbers of the first kind (cf.~\cite[subsection 24.1.3]{AS86}).

Then we have the following:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[{\bf (A)}] $f\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ if and only if 
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{sup-flbeta}
		P_{\alpha,\beta}\left(\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}\right)<\infty, &\mbox{for all}& \alpha,\beta,\ell\in \N_0,~~\mbox{such that}~~\ell\leq \beta,	\end{eqnarray}\label{fl-Schwartzkn}
		where $P_{\alpha,\beta}$ defines the seminorm defined by \eqref{seminormP}.
		\item[{\bf (B)}] $\left(\widetilde{f}_{\beta, \ell}\right)_{\ell\leq \beta}$ satisfies the recursive formula 
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{fl-Recursive}	\widetilde{f}_{\beta, \ell}=\begin{cases}
				f&,~\ell=0 \\
				\left((\beta-\ell) I+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\right)\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell-1}&,~\ell\geq 1.
			\end{cases} 
		\end{eqnarray}
	\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
First, we note that the proof of the statement {\bf (A)} follows from the statement {\bf (a)} of the Lemma \ref{Schwartz-Lemma} and from the set of inequalities
\begin{eqnarray*}
	\label{fl-Ineq}
	P_{\alpha,\beta}\left(\textcolor{blue}{f_{\ell}}\right)\leq 	P_{\alpha,\beta}\left(\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}\right)\leq \sum_{j=0}^\ell \sum_{m=0}^j \left|s(\ell,j)\right|\binom{j}{m}\beta^{j-m}~	P_{\alpha,\beta}\left(f_{m}\right),
\end{eqnarray*}
where $P_{\alpha,\beta}$ denotes the seminorm \eqref{seminormP} considered in Lemma \ref{Schwartz-Lemma}.

	For the proof of statement {\bf (B)}, let us denote by $(\lambda)_\ell$ ($\ell\in \N_0$) the falling factorials
	\begin{eqnarray}
\label{FallingFactorials}
	(\lambda)_{\ell}=
	\begin{cases}
		1 &,~\ell=0 \\
		\displaystyle \prod_{j=0}^{\ell-1}(\lambda-j)&,~\ell\geq 1. 
	\end{cases}
	\end{eqnarray}

	Using induction arguments over $\ell\in \N_0$, we prove that every $\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}$ admits the closed formula
	{\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{fl-FallingFactorials}
		{\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}(x)=	(-1)^\ell\left(-\beta I-\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\right)_{\ell}f(x), }& x\in \R,
	\end{eqnarray}}
		where $\left(-\beta I-\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\right)_{\ell}$ denotes the operator obtained by formally substituting  $\lambda\mapsto -\beta I-\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}$ on \eqref{FallingFactorials}.
	Combined with the combinatorial identity
	$$ (\lambda)_\ell=\sum_{j=0}^\ell s(\ell,j)\lambda^j,$$
which involves the signed Stirling numbers of the first kind, $s(\ell,j)$ (cf.~\cite[subsection 24.1.3]{AS86}), and the binomial formula, this shows that \eqref{fl-Stirling} satisfies the recursive formula \eqref{fl-Recursive}, as required.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{Fkn-Schwartz-Theorem}}\label{ProofTheorem1}
\begin{proof}
	We begin by recalling the intertwining properties 	\eqref{knFourierIntertwining}  (see \cite[Theorem 5.6]{BKO}).
	Consequently, it follows immediately that
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\left(n\left|~y~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}~\right)^{\alpha} \left(n\left|~y~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}~\right)^\beta (\mathcal{F}_{k,n}f)(y)=(-1)^{\alpha+\beta}\int_\R  h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)~B_{k,n}(x,y) d\mu_{k ,n}(x),
	\end{eqnarray*}
	holds for all $\alpha,\beta\in \N_0$, where
	$\left(h_{\alpha,\beta}\right)_{\alpha,\beta\in \N_0}$ denotes the sequence of functions defined by
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)&=&\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}~\right)^\alpha\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}~\right)^{\beta} f(x),~~~x\in\R\setminus \{0\}.
		%, \\
		%	\widetilde{g}_{\alpha, %\beta}^{(\ell)}(x)&=&\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}~\right)^{\beta}\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}^x~\right)^\alpha \widetilde{f}_{\ell}(x),~~~x \in \R,
	\end{eqnarray*}

	Thus, by the {\it uniformly boundedness} of the kernel $B_{k,n}(x,y)$ \eqref{Bcondition}, the inversion formula \eqref{FourierInversionIntegral}, and the intertwining property $\mathcal{F}_{k,n}\circ \mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}=-\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}\circ \mathcal{F}_{k,n}$ (which also follows from \eqref{knFourierIntertwining}), it suffices to verify that 
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{L1-integral}
		\int_\R \left|~h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)~\right| d\mu_{k ,n}(x)<\infty,
	\end{eqnarray}
	holds for each $f\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$.


	We first observe that the recursive relation
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)=\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}~\right)^{\alpha-\beta}h_{\beta,\beta}(x)&,~x\in \R\setminus \{0\},
	\end{eqnarray*}
	which holds for $\alpha>\beta$ allows us to focus on the case $\alpha \leq \beta$.

	If $\alpha=0$, the claim is immediate. Otherwise, by combining Lemma \ref{RecursiveLemma} and Lemma~\ref{Schwartz-Lemma}, one may show that $h_{1,\beta}\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$. Indeed, since $\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}f \in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$ and
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		h_{1,\beta}(x)=4\beta\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}~\right)^{\beta-1}\left((\beta-1) f(x)+\mathbb{H}_{k,\frac{2}{n}}f(x)\right)~\\+\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}~\right)^{\beta}\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}~\right)f(x), &x\in \R
	\end{eqnarray*}
	the desired result is obtained by applying the triangle inequality.

	For a general $\alpha\in \N_0$,~an inductive argument based on Lemma \ref{RecursiveLemma} and Lemma~\ref{Schwartz-Lemma} shows that there exist constants $\lambda_{\beta,\ell}\in \R$ ($0\leq \ell \leq \alpha \leq \beta$) such that 
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\label{h_alpha-beta}h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)=
		\displaystyle \sum_{\ell=0}^{\alpha}\lambda_{\beta,\ell}~\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}~\right)^{\beta-\alpha-\ell}\left(~n\left|~x~\right|^{2-\frac{2}{n}}\Delta_{k}^x~\right)^\ell \widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}(x)&,~x\in \R.
	\end{eqnarray}

	Here, the sequence $\left(\widetilde{f}_{\beta,\ell}\right)_{\ell \leq \beta}$ is defined as in Proposition \ref{Schwartz-Proposition}.
	The triangle inequality, when applied to the expansion \eqref{h_alpha-beta} directly yields $h_{\alpha,\beta}\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$. 
	Finally, in order to verify the condition \eqref{L1-integral}, we choose $\nu\in \N$ such that the integral 
	$$
	\sigma_{k,n}(\nu):=\int_{\R} \left(1+n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{-\nu}d\mu_{k ,n}(x)
	$$
	converges. Since $h_{\alpha,\beta}\in \mathcal{S}_{k,n}(\R)$, one has
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\label{supCond}
		\sup_{x\in \R \setminus\{0\}}\left(1+n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{\nu}\left|h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)\right|<\infty,& \mbox{for all}&\alpha,\beta\in \N_0.
	\end{eqnarray*}
	Hence,
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\label{L1-integralg}
		\int_\R \left|~h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)~\right| d\mu_{k ,n}(x) \leq \sigma_{k,n}(\nu) ~\sup_{x\in \R\setminus\{0\}}\left(1+n\left|~x~\right|^{\frac{2}{n}}\right)^{\nu}\left|h_{\alpha,\beta}(x)\right|,
	\end{eqnarray*}
	which verifies the condition \eqref{L1-integral} and completes the proof.
\end{proof}

