%Spatial audio reproduction aims to recreate immersive soundscapes, enabling virtual reality, augmented reality, and immersive teleconferencing applications. Traditional, standardized loudspeaker setups that are usually used to evaluate spatial audio reproduction algorithms are often impractical for home use. Media device orchestration offers a flexible alternative by utilizing a network of heterogeneous devices (e.g., laptops, smart speakers, smart phones etc.), but introduces synchronization challenges due to \ac{SRO}. Although network-based synchronization protocols, such as precision time protocol and network time protocol, have been explored, the impact of spatial audio reproduction in the presence of \ac{SRO} on a listener's perception remains under-investigated. In this work, we analyze the effect of simple stereo reproduction in the presence of \ac{SRO} on the listener's perception. We also propose an audio-domain \ac{SRO} compensation method that uses spatial filtering to isolate loudspeaker contributions. This is then used along with the playback signal to estimate the \ac{SRO}. The \ac{SRO} is then compensated before the reproduction. The proposed system is evaluated through objective performance metrics and subjective listening tests, demonstrating its effectiveness in maintaining spatial cues in the presence of \ac{SRO}.

