\section{Introduction}

%Spatial audio, also commonly known as immersive audio\,\cite{Rumsey_2001, Roginska_2017} enables a range of applications such as virtual/augmented reality, gaming, and immersive teleconferencing. 

Spatial audio capturing and reproduction enable a wide range of applications in entertainment and teleconferencing \cite{Rumsey_2001, Roginska_2017}. On the playback side, spatial audio reproduction aims to recreate the captured complex acoustic environments - or construct completely new ones - allowing listeners to perceive localized sounds from dedicated positions in space and to experience a sense of envelopment.

%Reproduction typically involves either binaural rendering of spatial formats such as Ambisonics\,\cite{Zotter_2019}, object-based audio\,\cite{Roginska_2017}, and channel-based audio\,\cite{Roginska_2017} for playback over headphones or stereo loudspeakers, or loudspeaker-based reproduction using amplitude panning techniques like vector-based amplitude panning (VBAP)\,\cite{Pulkki1997}. These techniques are often evaluated using standardized loudspeaker layout\,\cite{ITU-R-BS.775-3,ITU-R-BS.2159-6,ISO23091-3:2018} in controlled environments, where speakers are arranged at uniform distances around the listener. However, such setups are impractical in domestic settings due to cost and spatial constraints.

A key challenge in spatial audio reproduction over wirelessly connected loudspeakers is the synchronization of the individual playback devices. One important factor in this regard is clock skew caused by \acp{SRO} between devices with individual clocks. \acp{SRO} cause time-varying misalignment of playback signals, leading to degradation of spatial cues. Existing clock synchronization methods rely on network-based protocols such as \ac{PTP}\,\cite{ieee1588-2008} and \ac{NTP}\,\cite{rfc5905}, which aim to align device clocks\,\cite{Culbert_2006,Lauri2015}. However, to the best of our knowledge, the impact of the presence of \acp{SRO} on the listener's perception in spatial audio reproduction has not been systematically studied.

%To overcome these limitations, \ac{MDO}has emerged as a flexible approach\,\cite{Francombe_2017,Francombe_2018}, leveraging a network of heterogeneous devices—such as laptops, smart speakers, and smartphones—for collaborative spatial rendering. However, \ac{MDO} introduces synchronization challenges, particularly \acp{SRO} arising from the use of individual clocks. This leads to time-varying misalignment of playback signals and degradation of spatial cues. 

%Estimation of \ac{SRO} using only audio-domain observations have been extensively studied in applications using wireless sensor networks\,\cite{SMG_BSRO_2012,wang_CMSRO_WASN_2016,schmalenstroeer_multi-stage_2017,chinaev_online_2021,gburrek_synchronization_2022} and acoustic echo cancellation\,\cite{pawig_adaptive_2010,abe_frequency_2014,helwani_clock_2022,korse_2024}. However, to the best of our knowledge, no existing approaches have investigated \ac{SRO} compensated spatial audio rendering that work purely in audio-domain. 

While \ac{SRO} estimation using audio-domain observations has been extensively explored in wireless sensor networks\,\cite{SMG_BSRO_2012,wang_CMSRO_WASN_2016,schmalenstroeer_multi-stage_2017,chinaev_online_2021,gburrek_synchronization_2022} and acoustic echo cancellation\,\cite{pawig_adaptive_2010,abe_frequency_2014,helwani_clock_2022,korse_2024}, no prior work addresses audio-domain \ac{SRO} compensation for spatial audio reproduction specifically.

In this work, we examine the effects of \acp{SRO} in a stereo loudspeaker scenario. Further, we propose an audio-domain \ac{SRO} compensation method that applies spatial filtering\,\cite{VanVeen_1988} to isolate loudspeaker contributions.  The isolated loudspeaker signals are then used along with the original playback signals to estimate the \acp{SRO} using the~\ac{DWACD} algorithm~\cite{gburrek_synchronization_2022}. We then compensate for the effect of \acp{SRO} prior to the spatial rendering step, thus eliminating the need for explicit clock synchronization. Evaluation using objective metrics and subjective listening tests demonstrates that the proposed method preserves spatial cues and mitigates perceptual degradation.

