The mapping between the centrality of nodes and the centrality of objects for evaluation could be done in several different ways ordered from the simplest to more sophisticated:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item  
  \textit{first step:} find the closest node of the road network to the object; \\
  \textit{second step:} consider this result as an estimation of the centrality of the object.
  \item 
  \textit{first step:} create the buffer area of some radius around the object; \\
  \textit{second step:} calculate how much points of the road graph lie inside this area; \\
  \textit{third step:} calculate the mean value of the centrality among  these points; \\
  \textit{fourth step:} consider this result as the estimation of the centrality of the object. 
  \item Also, we can do the extra calculations – interpolate over nodes of the road graph.  Interpolation is the method of finding unknown values of some function in intermediate points using a discrete set of known values of a function at some points. 
\end{enumerate}

We implement the third approach because after the interpolation we get the smooth continuous surface of centrality values at any point. It allows to estimate the centrality of our objects more precisely compared to other mentioned possibilities. For interpolation, we use the method of basis functions. In this method, we get estimation as a linear combination of basis functions ~\citep{Demyanov2010,Buhmann2000,Regis2006,Zhang2024}. 

\begin{equation}
    Z(x_0) = \sum_{i=1}^{n}c_iB(h_{0i})
    \label{eq:basis_func}
\end{equation}

where $Z(x_0)$ - estimation at the point $x_0$; $h_{0i}$ - distance from point $x_0$ to point $x_i$; $B(h_{0i})$ - basis function that depends on the distance; $c_i$ - weight coefficients. Coefficient $c_i$ defines algebraic sign of an element and its contribution.

We use the linear spline function as the basis function, which has the form:

\begin{equation}
   B(h) = -h.
   \label{eq:basis_func_linear_spline}
\end{equation}

The weight coefficients $c_i$ are derived from the condition that at known points the results of interpolation must coincide with known values, i.e. the interpolation model in all points $(x_i, y_i)$ must estimate values equal to $V(x_i, y_i)$. Thus we need to solve the system of $N$ linear equations with $N$ unknown variables. 

%\begin{equation}
   \begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
      \sum_{i=1}^{N}c_i\left[B(h(x_i,y_i,x_1,y_1))\right] = V(x_1,y_1) \\
      ... \\
      \sum_{i=1}^{N}c_i\left[B(h(x_i,y_i,x_N,y_N))\right] = V(x_N,y_N)
    \end{cases}\
\end{equation}
    \label{eq:basis_func_linear_spline_sys}
%\end{equation}

To do this we use the function RBFInterpolator from Python library "SciPy". The results of interpolation for Vladivostok are shown in Figure ~\ref{fig:fig7} (b). Here for the centrality of the objects we use the term “Development of the road network” underlining our mathematical interpretation of this concept.

