On the base of the described results, we can state that our model is effective and interpretable. As we've already said the key factor for the identification of the price of a land parcel is its location. We see that linear regression does relatively well but the price of an object can include latent (local-territorial) characteristics. In order to take this into account we tried to apply a powerful instrument dealing with spatially distributed data – geostatistics. Geostatistical methods allow to model the distribution of objects in space. The central task of geostatistics is to reconstruct a studied phenomenon on the base of the values calculated in a limited number of points ~\citep{Demyanov2010}. Mathematically we can contemplate this problem as an interpolation problem. For getting the best spatial estimations kriging methods are used. Estimations obtained by kriging have a minimal variation of error. Thus kriging is the best estimator in a class of linear interpolators ~\citep{Demyanov2010}. There are two basic conditions for using these methods – data should have spatial continuity and stationarity ~\citep{Kovalevskiy2012}. The first condition means that two points located close together should have rather similar values than points separated more. The second condition means that in some locality the studied phenomenon must be spatially homogeneous.

We can check the condition of stationarity using the probability density function of a variable, in our case we analyze the distribution of residuals of our linear model. If the histogram is similar to the Gaussian distribution then in all likelihood there is  stationarity (statistical homogeneity). Strictly speaking, this criteria is not a sufficient condition for confirmation of stationarity but it is strong enough and sufficient condition for practical work ~\citep{Kovalevskiy2012}. Figure ~\ref{fig:fig15} and the result of the Jarque–Bera test confirm the stationarity in  linear model residuals. It’s worth noting that before checking data on stationarity we need to examine whether data contains a trend. In our case, we removed the trend from data using linear regression and then we modeled its residuals with geostatistical methods. The trend in data can distort the variogram –  an instrument for estimation of spatial correlations in data ~\citep{Demyanov2010,Isaaks1989}. The variogram model is a function describing a dependence between the studied spatial variable and the distance. If data contains a trend then a variogram would reproduce a large-scale trend that leads to missing a correlation of the observed variable on a small scale.

Also yet one important criteria (but also not sufficient) of stationarity is that the variogram should have a sill beginning  at some distance. For building a variogram the researcher can choose the fitting curve from a list of basic models of variogram. In this paper, we used the exponential model, which is shown in Figure ~\ref{fig:fig17}.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{images/figure17.jpg}
    \caption{Fitting by exponential model}
    \label{fig:fig17}
\end{figure}

The variogram is used in finding the estimations of ordinary kriging ~\citep{Demyanov2010,Isaaks1989} and substituted instead of covariation function in the process of minimizing the Lagrangian that allows to get interpolation coefficients of ordinary kriging taking into account imposed on them constraints ~\citep{Demyanov2010}.

The second condition for implementing kriging – the spatial continuity. We checked it by plotting the correlation of points as a function of the distance between them. From Figure ~\ref{fig:fig18} we see that with increasing distance between points, the correlation decreases. Therefore this criteria is satisfied.

\begin{figure}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/figure18.png}
    \caption{Checking of the spatial continuity of the data}
    \label{fig:fig18}
\end{figure}

Thus the final value of the per square meter price is the sum of the trend that we got using the linear regression and the prediction of linear model residuals that we got using kriging.

The last step of modeling is getting the resulting predicted estimations – we need to take the exponent of the predicted logarithmic value.

We assess the result by comparison of fitting errors using cross-validation. The average fitting error for linear regression is 28,36\% and for linear regression with kriging is 19,23\%. Also in Figure ~\ref{fig:fig19} predicted vs. actual plots are shown. Both clouds are arranged along the diagonal line but for regression-kriging it has a more elongated shape corresponding to lower errors.

