Sometimes the data can include non-linear dependencies that make a linear regression ineffective. In such a situation, we can try other algorithms that work better with non-linear  dependencies in data. The most popular method is the “Decision tree” ~\citep{DiazRamirez2023}. This algorithm forms the hierarchic tree structure that consists of rules “If … then …”. The rules are generated on the base of the training dataset in the process of training. The rules of the algorithm are created on natural language and they are simple for understanding. This is an advantage of the algorithm. But also method has significant shortage – it is prone to overfitting. It means that generated rules will fit only the training dataset, this tree will have bad results on other datasets. The extension of this algorithm called “Random Forest” ~\citep{Breiman2017} rectifies this shortcoming. This method creates a set of decision trees. The  advantage of it is the high accuracy of prediction and also this algorithm tends to work well with hyperparameters by default. Compared to the “Decision tree” the “Random Forest” is much less prone to overfitting. The shortage of this method is the complicated interpretation of modeling results due to the large number of generated rules.

Yet one algorithm that predicts with good accuracy and possesses better interpretability than “Random Forest” is “RuleFit” ~\citep{Molnar2022,Masis2021}. The underlying concept of the method is a combination of linear regression and predicted models that are based on decision trees. This model was developed in 2005 and was positioned as a sparse linear regression model (a class of models that after the training can have coefficients with zero weights). This algorithm detects the effects of interaction between features in the form of decision rules. RuleFit automatically generates these functions (rules) from decision trees. Using  RuleFit we can choose any algorithm that generates the set of decision trees, for example, random forest or gradient boosting. Generated rules are used in the linear regression model as individual variables. The base model of the algorithm is LASSO (Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator also known as L1 regularization) ~\citep{Tibshirani1996}, because it allows the selection of the most significant rules from the set of rules  for including them in the final model. LASSO is similar to ordinary linear regression but there is a supplementary component of regularization in the optimization functional of the model. This component is the penalty for the complexity of the model. Mathematically we can write this as:

\begin{equation}
   RSS+\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{k}|\widehat{\beta_{i}}| \rightarrow min_{\widehat{\beta_{i}}}
   \label{eq:lasso}
\end{equation}

where $RSS$ - Residual Sum of Squares; $\lambda$ - regularization parameter; $\widehat{\beta_{i}}$ - coefficients if models. The goal of minimization is reaching a compromise between errors of regression and the dimension of space of features. Therefore RuleFit is a LASSO regression that takes as input both individual factors and the rules created by the random forest algorithm. These rules describe the interplay of variables. The distinctive characteristic of  LASSO is going to zero of some factors as a result of minimization. This procedure determines the most informative factors. The tendency of some factors to disappear is controlled with a regularization parameter that is fitted using cross-validation (procedure of empiric estimation of the generalization ability of the algorithm). More information about adjusting the regularization parameter can be found here ~\citep{Friedman2010}. Due to the fact that the algorithm automatically selects the most significant factors the additional checking of selected factors is not required. Unlike the Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) method, the LASSO (and consequently RuleFit) does not have any prerequisites. We can estimate the effectiveness of the model by using common evaluation metrics such as $MSE$ (Mean Squared Error), $MAE$ (Mean Absolute Error), $\bar{A}$ (Average approximation error), and $R^2$ (Coefficient of determination).

\begin{equation}
   MSE=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}e_{i}^{2}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(y_i-\hat{y_i})^2
   \label{eq:mse}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
   MAE=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}|e_{i}|=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}|y_i-\hat{y_i}|
   \label{eq:mae}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
   \bar{A}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{|y_i-\hat{y_i}|}{y_i}*100\%
   \label{eq:avg_err}
\end{equation}

For building the RuleFit model we used both binary and continuous features. As continuous features, we used the factors from the correlation matrix (Figure ~\ref{fig:fig11}) where correlation with the dependent variable is 0,15 and more. Factors satisfying this are:

