This model contains both the factors describing the basic characteristics of a building – aggregated variables of the year built and the number of storeys, wall materials – allowing to differentiate one building from another, and factors characterizing the location of building – “Distance to the seacoast”, “Distance to the closest railway station”, “Distance to the water objects”, “Number of cafes”, “Distance to the town hall”. Also, the model distinguishes the individual characteristics of a flat within the building including features such as “Area of flat”, “Storey of flat: the first”, and “Storey of flat: the last”. We can note that the majority of rules include factors “Distance to the town hall (distance by the roads)”, “Aggregate: Year built \& Number of storeys”, and “Area of flat”. It can be explained by the fact that Vladivostok is a highly diverse city with an entwinement of historical and modern buildings and RuleFit tries to differentiate them. This is the typical example where linear regression performs poorly and tackling non-linear dependencies is required.

In order to evaluate the effectiveness of RuleFit prediction we can turn to Figure ~\ref{fig:fig23} (b) where the comparison of predicted and real values of per square meter price is shown. If we compare  Figure ~\ref{fig:fig23} (a) and Figure ~\ref{fig:fig23} (b) we can see that the cloud of points for Rulefit is more elongated along the diagonal line. It means that the residual variance of RuleFit is less than for OLS regression. Additionally we can note that RuleFit still underestimates high prices but performs better for low prices.

