%\end{figure}

Unfortunately, RuleFit like any other model has its drawbacks. First, as one can see in Table ~\ref{tab:tab5}, RuleFit sometimes can keep strange rules that are very similar to each other.  This is a drawback of the generation of decision trees by the algorithm and it complicates the interpretation of the model. Second, due to the stochastic nature of the tree's generation process, RuleFit each time will generate new rules. This problem can be solved by setting the state of the random number generator. Additionally the same randomness will lead to a different set of final rules after changing the training data for the model. Thus the RuleFit is a badly reproduced model and because of it, we evaluate the final results of the model on the whole set of data without splitting it on test and train. But in the process of study we did such splitting and made crossvalidation which gives the average $R_{adj}^2$ to be 0,58. We present the result of the RuleFit model in Table ~\ref{tab:tab6}.

