\begin{abstract}
We have studied $^{32}$Si and $^{29}$Al using $^{12}$C($^{22}$Ne,2p) and $^{12}$C($^{22}$Ne,$\alpha$p) fusion-evaporation reactions.  In both cases, we observed significant population of high-spin structures distinct from the ground-state yrast bands.  In $^{32}$Si, most of the high-energy states feed into a $J^{\pi} = 5^-$ nanosecond isomer.  In $^{29}$Al, we identified a rotor-like negative-parity band with a $J^{\pi} = 7/2^-$ band-head. Doppler shift lifetime measurements were performed for all observed states. These results were compared to shell model calculations and interpreted in terms of proton and neutron cross-shell excitation.
