\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}

Electronic bridge mechanisms driving the \textsuperscript{229}Th  nuclear clock transition  in the vacuum-ultraviolet-transparent crystals \textsuperscript{229}Th:LiCAF (LiCaAlF$_6$) and \textsuperscript{229}Th:LiSAF (LiSrAlF$_6$) are investigated theoretically. Due to doping-induced symmetry breaking within the host crystal, electronic defect states emerge around the thorium nucleus and can facilitate nuclear (de)excitation via laser-assisted electronic bridge mechanisms. We investigate spontaneous and laser-assisted electronic bridge schemes  for different charge compensation mechanisms. 
While the calculated spontaneous electronic bridge rates are very small, laser-assisted electronic bridge schemes for nuclear (de)excitation turn out to be significantly more efficient than both spontaneous nuclear decay and direct laser excitation, offering  promising prospects for the future clock operation. 







\end{abstract}
