The first equation we test is Burgers' equation:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
         &\f{\p U}{\p t} + \f{\p f(U)}{\p x} = 0\csp x\in[0,1], \\
        & U_0(x,y_1, y_2) = \LRp{1+0.5y_1}\sin(2\pi x) + y_2, 
    \end{split}
     \label{eq:Burgers}
\end{equation}
where $y_1,y_2\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1].$  
%For Burgers' equation, the wave speed estimates $\lambda^-, \lambda^+$ are equal to the left and right reconstructed states $\Tilde{u}_L, \Tilde{u}_R$. \ST{This is not very infomative}
We impose periodic boundary conditions in the spatial domain $D_x$ and outflow boundary condition in the stochastic domain $D_y$. The simulation is run until the final time $T=0.2$, when the solution develops a shock  propagating towards the right. Note that uncertainty is introduced in both the amplitude of the initial sine wave and the shock speed.

We use SFV method to solve the problem with both reconstruction methods described in Section \ref{sec:WENO} and compare the relative errors in \autoref{tb:Burgers1}. In this comparison, we fix $N_x=64$ and vary $N_y$. The solution from state reconstruction is taken as the reference to assess the accuracy of flux reconstruction. The results demonstrate third-order convergence, indicating that state and flux reconstruction have similar accuracy. 

Next, we evaluate the performance of the ROM. We fix $N_x=64$ and $N_y = 32^2$. First, we plot the error between SFV method with flux reconstruction and the ROM for different numbers of modes in \autoref{fig:Burgers_er1}, observing that the error decreases as $N$ increases. Then, we fix $N=50$ and vary $N_H\geq N$ to assess the accuracy of hyper-reduction in \autoref{fig:Burgers_er2}, where we observe a convergence of error as $N_H$ increases. However, the error at $N_H=N$ is already reasonable, especially considering the significant gain in computational efficiency.


Finally, we plot the solution $U$ at the final time for both flux reconstruction and ROM at $y_1 = 1$ and $y_2 = 1$ in \autoref{fig:Burgers_sol}. Here we set $N=N_H$ and consider $N=N_H = 10$ and $N=N_H=20$. Next, we plot the solution means and standard deviations for these two mode choices in \autoref{fig:Burgers_mean}. We observe oscillations around shocks for ROM at $N=10$, which diminishes as $N$ increases to 20. Similar oscillations appear in the solution mean and standard deviation at $N=10$, but they are also reduced at $N=20.$ 



